{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cghm7j8LV4dPEfwbH6bQt","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01TpJQZngMwRUCR62pMTSqLV","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build__20261104-035148__xe1b7e1/work/PLAN.md","offset":1200,"limit":280},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":12613,"cache_read_input_tokens":54478,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":12613},"output_tokens":53,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6eff3345-97e6-4e65-b411-5b68a8aed193","uuid":"f429d4b8-a6bf-4b08-93d2-2b96cbf1445b","timestamp":"2026-11-04T10:52:14.747Z","request_id":"req_011Cghm7ishbt2qTZmSe9mrU","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01TpJQZngMwRUCR62pMTSqLV":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build__20261104-035148__xe1b7e1/work/PLAN.md","offset":1200,"limit":280}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TpJQZngMwRUCR62pMTSqLV","type":"tool_result","content":"1200\t| `truck` | flower lorry | 65–85 | 0.30 | potted plants; 2 lengths |\n1201\t| `tractor` | tractor | 35–50 | 0.24 | farm stages only; blinking orange light |\n1202\t| `scooter` | scooter | 80–100 | 0.10 | rider rig |\n1203\t| `icecream` | ice-cream truck | 60–75 | 0.24 | plays its jingle within 3,000 units |\n1204\t\n1205\tDensities by stage come from §8.2's mix weights × the base density 0.32 (vehicles per 10,000 units per lane). Hard\n1206\tstages add 0.05.\n1207\t\n1208\t**Roadside sets per kit.** These are sprites. Core props are `E.prop(name)`, store and own shapes are `E.shapeSprite`,\n1209\tand ★ marks what we draw new.\n1210\t\n1211\t| Kit | Ground and strips | Scenery |\n1212\t|---|---|---|\n1213\t| beach | sand and water | palm, ★umbrella, ★beach hut, ★lifeguard tower, ★surf rack, rock, ★buoy, ★Ferris-wheel pier |\n1214\t| fields | `rows` in lavender or sunflower colours | ★cypress, bush, ★farmhouse, ★beehives, ★silo, ★barn, ★wind turbine (animated), ★hay bale, fence |\n1215\t| cliffs | cliff edge and rail | rock (store `kenney-nature` cliff pieces), ★lemon tree, ★terraced house cluster, ★chapel, sign |\n1216\t| lake | water and pines | pine, ★boathouse, ★paddle steamer (moving), ★truss bridge (strip and spans), ★waterfall (animated) |\n1217\t| canyon | rock and sand | ★mesa pillar, ★rock arch (span), cactus in flower (★ a variant of the core cactus), rock, ★souvenir stand |\n1218\t| alpine | snow and grass | ★cherry tree (blossom, petals), pine, ★chalet, ★cow, ★snow shed (span), rock |\n1219\t| town | cobble and facades | ★arcade facade (strip), awning, ★market stall, lantern, streetlamp, ★fountain, ★cathedral dome (landmark), banners (bunting span) |\n1220\t| vineyard | `rows` of grapes | ★cypress, ★villa, barrel, ★castle on a hill (landmark), ★wine cart, fence |\n1221\t| lagoon | water both sides, low rails | ★flamingo (flock animation), ★salt pyramid, ★lift bridge, lamp |\n1222\t| jungle | grass and wet | palm, ★giant leaf, ★fern, ★ruin arch (span), ★orchid (emissive), ★waterfall, ★parrot |\n1223\t| night city | facades and a neon strip | ★neon sign set (6 messages), ★yacht, ★casino flower (landmark), streetlamp (lit), ★funicular |\n1224\t| night desert | sand | cactus, ★night-bloom cactus (emissive), ★motel sign, ★radio dish, rock |\n1225\t| highlands | heather and stone walls | ★standing stone, ★castle ruin, rock, bush (heather tint), ★sheep |\n1226\t\n1227\t**Request objects** (★ all): heart balloon (3 colours), gnome (googly eyes), bee swarm (Blobs with `wings: true`), ribbon\n1228\t(a pink strip between 2 cars), cone, flower arch, bouquet, photo box (asphalt paint) with a photographer rig, gate\n1229\tflowers in 5 colours (Memory Lane).\n1230\t\n1231\t**Signs** (★): the fork sign (two names, arrows, flowers), CHECKPOINT and GOAL arches, ROAD CLOSED barriers, the\n1232\twind-sock warning, the stage name boards, and billboards with original fake ads: \"Gelato Gelatissimo\", \"Fioretti — Born\n1233\tto Bloom\", \"Radio Petal FM\", \"Hotel Vespera\". Billboards are written in the pixel font.\n1234\t\n1235\t### 8.7 Love in Bloom requests (all 14)\n1236\t\n1237\tThresholds are scaled by the `requestDifficulty` tuning. A miss gives 0 petals and a mood drop.\n1238\t\n1239\t| Type | Call-out (card) | Setup in the window | Measure | Perfect / Great / Good |\n1240\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n1241\t| `pass` | PASS {n} CARS! | traffic ×1.6; n is 6–10 | clean overtakes | ≥ n / ≥ .75n / ≥ .5n |\n1242\t| `drift` | DRIFT FOR ME! | the window covers the stage's twistiest part | drift score against target T (2 long drifts) | ≥ T / ≥ .7T / ≥ .4T |\n1243\t| `ribbons` | CUT THE RIBBONS! | k = 4–6 ribbon pairs | snapped / k | 100 % / ≥ 70 % / ≥ 40 % |\n1244\t| `balloons` | POP THE BALLOONS! | 10–16 balloons at car height in lines and zigzags | popped / total | ≥ 90 % / ≥ 70 % / ≥ 45 % |\n1245\t| `gnomes` | BONK THE GNOMES! | 8–12 gnomes standing on the road | bonked / total | ≥ 90 % / ≥ 70 % / ≥ 45 % |\n1246\t| `bees` | DODGE THE BEES! | 6–10 swarms crossing lanes on sine paths | hits (a hit stings: a wobble and a mood drop) | 0 / 1 / 2 |\n1247\t| `cones` | SLALOM THE CONES! | 8–12 cones on a line | cones passed on the correct alternating side | ≥ 90 % / ≥ 70 % / ≥ 45 % |\n1248\t| `arches` | THROUGH THE ARCHES! | 6–8 one-lane flower arches that step between lanes | passed / total | 100 % / ≥ 75 % / ≥ 50 % |\n1249\t| `speed` | KEEP IT OVER {v}! | v is 230–260 (in the player's units) | share of the window above v | ≥ 95 % / ≥ 80 % / ≥ 60 % |\n1250\t| `clean` | NOT A SCRATCH! | a window of about 15 s with dense traffic | contacts (bumps, crashes, scrapes) | 0 / — / 1 |\n1251\t| `draft` | STAY IN THE DRAFT! | traffic queues to draft behind | seconds of slipstream above 0.5 | ≥ 6 / ≥ 4 / ≥ 2 |\n1252\t| `close` | CLOSE CALLS! | denser traffic | near misses | ≥ n / ≥ .7n / ≥ .4n (n is 5–6) |\n1253\t| `bouquets` | GRAB THE BOUQUETS! | 10–14 bouquets, some on the inside of bends and beside traffic | collected / total | ≥ 90 % / ≥ 70 % / ≥ 45 % |\n1254\t| `photo` | STOP FOR A PHOTO! | a painted box, 2 segments long, at a landmark, with a photographer | **Perfect:** below 50 km/h with the car wholly inside the box and under 3.0 s lost. **Great:** stopped in the box. **Good:** through the box below 100 km/h. | — |\n1255\t\n1256\tThe photo stop costs clock time, a deliberate risk against the reward. Juni and Vivi say a different line for it.\n1257\t\n1258\t### 8.8 The Coast 2 Coast Tour missions (14)\n1259\t\n1260\t| # | Giver · title | Route | Rules (`OB.Rules` kind) | C / B / A / AAA | Seeds (base) and unlock |\n1261\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n1262\t| B1 | Bruno · **First Light Sprint** | S1 to its checkpoint | `sprint`: reach it with the clock from 52 s | 0 s / 5 s / 9 s / 13 s left | 150 |\n1263\t| B2 | Bruno · **Drift Duel** | S2 | `driftduel` against Kiki: more drift points by the stage's end. Live points bar for both. | win / +10 % / +25 % / +45 % margin | 200 · the Gardenia bed |\n1264\t| B3 | Bruno · **Draft Dash** | S4 | `draftdash` against the Bramble Brothers: slipstream points (seconds × speed) plus finishing first | win / +10 % / +25 % / +40 % | 250 |\n1265\t| B4 | Bruno · **Last One Wilts** | S5 → S8 → S12, then Goal B | `knockout`: 5 racers (you, Kiki, Brambles, Lustres, Ottavio). The last at each checkpoint is out; finish first. | top 2 / 1st / 1st +2 s / 1st +5 s | 300 · the \"Lemon Drop Rush\" bed |\n1266\t| M1 | Marisol · **Balloon Bonanza** | S1 | `collect` balloons: 40 spread over the stage | 20 / 26 / 32 / 38 | 150 |\n1267\t| M2 | Marisol · **Gnome Patrol** | S9 | `collect` gnomes (24) with bees: a bee hit costs 2 | 10 / 14 / 18 / 22 net | 200 · the Picnic Gingham outfit |\n1268\t| M3 | Marisol · **Memory Lane** | S2 | `memory`: she shows a 5-colour flower sequence (2 s per colour), then 5 gate sets with 3 coloured gates each; drive through the right colour | 3 / 4 / 5 / 5 with no hints shown | 250 · the \"Moonflower\" bed |\n1269\t| V1 | Vivi · **Not a Scratch** | S8 | `clean`: zero contacts, ranked by time | under par +12 s / +6 s / +2 s / under par | 200 |\n1270\t| V2 | Vivi · **Fashionably Fast** | S10 | `speedkeep`: above 240 km/h for the stage, in crosswinds | 70 % / 80 % / 88 % / 95 % | 250 · the Magnolia bed |\n1271\t| V3 | Vivi · **Heartbreakers** | S11 → Goal A | `heartbreak` against Lance and Lacey: both couples start with 20 petals. Every 2 s behind them drains 1, every pass steals 2 (both ways), and theirs drain the same. End with more than 0, or make theirs 0. | > 0 / ≥ 10 / ≥ 18 / theirs at 0 | 300 · the Gala Gown outfit |\n1272\t| J1 | Juni · **Close Calls** | S3 | `count` near misses | 8 / 12 / 15 / 20 | 150 |\n1273\t| J2 | Juni · **Ribbon Cutter** | S7 | `count` ribbons: 16 pairs | 8 / 11 / 14 / 16 | 200 · the Rally Suit outfit |\n1274\t| J3 | Juni · **The Perfect Shot** | S6 | `photo3`: 3 photo boxes; the score is the sum of photo ratings | 1 Good each / 2 Great / 3 Great / 3 Perfect | 250 · the Mirror Roads bed |\n1275\t| F | Bruno and all · **Coast 2 Coast** | the full pyramid, forks open | `love`: Love in Bloom rules with all request types, and the passenger you choose | grade B / A / AA / AAA at any goal | 500 · the Girasole bed, the credits |\n1276\t\n1277\tEvery rival mission shows the rival's portrait and a live gap on the HUD.\n1278\t\n1279\t### 8.9 The Garage Garden: beds and costs\n1280\t\n1281\t| Bed | Seeds | Bed | Seeds |\n1282\t|---|---|---|---|\n1283\t| Gardenia GT Targa | 600 | Song \"Lemon Drop Rush\" | 300 |\n1284\t| Magnolia V12 Barchetta | 900 | Song \"Moonflower\" | 300 |\n1285\t| Girasole Hyper Spider | 1500 | Paint packs ×4 | 100 each |\n1286\t| Primavera Bloom Edition | 400 | Outfits ×3 | 150 each |\n1287\t| Orchidea Bloom Edition | 500 | Mirror Roads | 800 |\n1288\t\n1289\tThat totals **6,150 seeds**. All 14 missions at A (base 3,350 × 1.5) give 5,025. Arcade runs give about 150–350 each, so\n1290\tthe economy test's \"everything within 14 A-ranks plus 6 runs\" holds.\n1291\t\n1292\t### 8.10 Music (all original, all in code)\n1293\t\n1294\tRadio stations, in dial order:\n1295\t\n1296\t| # | Station | Song | BPM · key | Style and instruments | Progression (per 2 bars) | Hook idea |\n1297\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n1298\t| 1 | 88.6 PETAL FM | **Sunburst Samba** | 128 · D major | Latin fusion: congas, timbales, slap bass, brass stabs, steel-drum lead, e-piano comping | Dmaj7 · Bm7 · Em7 · A7sus4–A7 | the steel drum climbs a 6th and falls by step, with a timbale fill into each chorus |\n1299\t| 2 | 91.2 SUN FM | **Coastline Crush** | 116 · F major | Balearic pop-funk: four on the floor, a chorus-guitar skank, finger bass, a soft pulse lead, pad | Fmaj7 · Em7 · Dm7 · Gm7–C9 | a breezy 3-note pickup into a long held note |\n1300\t| 3 | 94.8 SHORE FM | **Shuffle on the Shore** | 110 shuffle (swing in 12/8 feel, steps 3) · B♭ major | jazz shuffle: organ, walking bass, brushed snare, sax lead | B♭6 · G7 · Cm7 · F7 (turnaround) | call and response between the sax and the organ |\n1301\t| 4 | 99.1 VESPA FM | **Vespa Vendetta** | 152 · A minor | Eurobeat: octave saw bass, supersaw stabs, fast 16th arps, a bright lead | Am · F · G · E7 | the chorus jumps an octave on beat 3 |\n1302\t| 5 | 103.5 PASTEL FM | **Pastel Highway** | 104 · E♭ major | city pop: DX-style e-piano, slap bass, sax or lead, claps on 2 and 4, pad | A♭maj7 · Gm7 · Fm7 · B♭7sus4 | a sax lick answered by e-piano chords |\n1303\t| 6 | 106.3 ZEST FM (unlock) | **Lemon Drop Rush** | 140 · G major | acid jazz into breakbeat: breakbeat drums, a rubbery 303-ish bass (resonant filter env), organ stabs | G7 · C7 · G7 · D7–C7 | an organ riff in 4ths |\n1304\t| 7 | 108.8 MOON FM (unlock) | **Moonflower** | 92 · C♯ minor | a night synth ballad: a pad, marimba arps, a soft lead, a slow kick | C♯m9 · Amaj7 · E · B | a long lead line with a slide |\n1305\t\n1306\tOther music:\n1307\t\n1308\t| Song | Use | Notes |\n1309\t|---|---|---|\n1310\t| **OutBloom!** | title | 120, C major; a brass fanfare into a samba groove |\n1311\t| **Garden Party** | menus, the Tour, the garden | 100 bossa nova; e-piano and nylon pluck |\n1312\t| **Petal Count** | results | 112; a light funk loop |\n1313\t| **Last Light Lullaby** | the credits and Bloom endings | 84 |\n1314\t\n1315\tJingles (played with `sting`):\n1316\t\n1317\t| Jingle | Feel |\n1318\t|---|---|\n1319\t| start fanfare | 2 bars |\n1320\t| checkpoint | a bright V–I |\n1321\t| request start | a rising triplet |\n1322\t| request success | a major arpeggio up |\n1323\t| request fail | the trombone slide |\n1324\t| fork tick | — |\n1325\t| Full Bloom | a sparkle sweep |\n1326\t| goal fanfare | 4 bars |\n1327\t| new record | — |\n1328\t| unlock bloom | — |\n1329\t| game over | \"Sunset Blues\", a slow minor-blues turnaround |\n1330\t\n1331\tEvery song needs its named layers and a `sparkle` layer (§7.14).\n1332\t\n1333\t### 8.11 Writing style and the line bank\n1334\t\n1335\t**Style.**\n1336\t- Short, sunny, cheeky, like a peppy arcade passenger who is half sweetheart, half backseat driver.\n1337\t- Exclamation-heavy but never mean. A touch of cheese and a wink. No profanity.\n1338\t- **Lengths:** call-outs ≤ 20 characters, in caps. Spoken lines ≤ 40 characters, 1–2 per bubble.\n1339\t- Banners are 1–3 words. Ending captions are a postcard sentence.\n1340\t- Each character's voice stays distinct: Marisol bubbly, Vivi theatrical (\"Darling…\"), Juni breathless slang, Bruno\n1341\t  booming with \"champ\".\n1342\t- **Required counts:** each passenger has ≥ 4 variants for each reaction (request intro per type, Perfect, Great, Good,\n1343\t  Miss, bump, crash, near miss, air, fork preference left and right, checkpoint, low time, goal, start). Bruno has ≥ 3\n1344\t  per event.\n1345\t\n1346\t**Seed lines** (use them, then write the rest in the same voice):\n1347\t\n1348\t| Who | Event | Lines |\n1349\t|---|---|---|\n1350\t| Marisol | start | \"Coast to coast? With you? Let's GO!\" |\n1351\t| Marisol | balloons | \"Ooh, balloons! Pop them all for me!\" |\n1352\t| Marisol | Perfect | \"Eee! You're amazing!\" · \"Again, again!\" |\n1353\t| Marisol | Miss | \"Aww… you missed so many.\" |\n1354\t| Marisol | crash | \"Kyaaa! My hair!\" |\n1355\t| Marisol | fork left | \"Left! I want to smell the lavender!\" |\n1356\t| Marisol | low time | \"Hurry, the sun's slipping!\" |\n1357\t| Vivi | start | \"Darling, do try not to dent anything.\" |\n1358\t| Vivi | speed | \"Faster. I refuse to arrive second.\" |\n1359\t| Vivi | Great | \"Hmm. Adequate. Barely.\" |\n1360\t| Vivi | Perfect | \"Now THAT is how one arrives!\" |\n1361\t| Vivi | bump | \"Was that a… car? Unacceptable.\" |\n1362\t| Vivi | fork right | \"Right, the cliffs. Everyone who matters goes that way.\" |\n1363\t| Juni | start | \"Okay okay okay, get me some ACTION!\" |\n1364\t| Juni | close | \"Closer! Closer! I need the shot!\" |\n1365\t| Juni | near miss | \"WHOA, did you see that?!\" |\n1366\t| Juni | Miss | \"Boooring. Give me danger!\" |\n1367\t| Juni | Perfect | \"Best shot of my LIFE!\" |\n1368\t| Juni | air | \"We're FLYING!\" |\n1369\t| Bruno | start | \"Morning, champ! The west coast is waiting!\" |\n1370\t| Bruno | drift | \"Ho ho! You drift like my rally days!\" |\n1371\t| Bruno | mission win | \"Mamma mia, what a finish!\" |\n1372\t| Bruno | mission fail | \"Again, champ! Even I spun out in '79.\" |\n1373\t| Announcer | — | \"CHECKPOINT!\" · \"TIME EXTENDED!\" · \"GOAL!\" · \"FULL BLOOM!\" · \"PERFECT START!\" · \"NEW RECORD!\" |\n1374\t\n1375\tEnding captions (one per goal and mood), for example: \"GOAL A · LANTERN PIER: A thousand lanterns, and one just for\n1376\tyou two.\" · \"GOAL E · FINISTERRA LIGHTHOUSE: The green flash. Best. Day. Ever.\"\n1377\t\n1378\tHelp, briefs and the How to Play text use the same voice, plainly.\n1379\t\n1380\t---\n1381\t\n1382\t## 9. Art direction and asset recipes\n1383\t\n1384\t### 9.1 Rules\n1385\t\n1386\t- **Base style.** The engine's native style: pixel art at 480×270, shaded with `E.tones` (cool shadows, warm lights, a\n1387\t  `hi` sparkle). Everything is drawn with `E.px.*`, `E.font`, sprites and shapes. Never use `ctx.arc`, `stroke`,\n1388\t  `fillText` or canvas gradients on the buffer. Translucency goes through `px.blend` or the dither helpers.\n1389\t- **Fog and distance.** Use the road module's fog (colour per time of day). Far sprites fade to the fog colour at the\n1390\t  module's stepped alpha.\n1391\t- **Palettes.** Every kit has five swatches: `ground`, `ground2`, `accent`, `rock` and `sky` ramps. Time of day applies\n1392\t  a grade on top: dawn peach and lilac, noon clean and saturated, late afternoon gold, sunset magenta to orange, night\n1393\t  indigo with neon.\n1394\t- **Sizes.**\n1395\t  - **The player's car** is about 128 px wide at the screen's bottom (480 wide).\n1396\t  - **Traffic** is drawn from the same model scale.\n1397\t  - **Palms** at the edge reach about 160 px tall near the camera.\n1398\t  - **Rigs in the car:** heads about 12–14 px, with the upper body showing.\n1399\t  - **Ending rigs** are about 70 px tall (size 1.6).\n1400\t\n1401\t### 9.2 Sky presets (`OB.Sky`, in `01-data-world.js`)\n1402\t\n1403\tEach sky is `{ gradient: [5 colours], sun | moon: { x, y, r, color, halo }, clouds: { kind, color, y, speed },\n1404\tlayers: [...] }`.\n1405\t\n1406\t- **Layer kinds:** the engine's `sea`, `hills`, `mountains` (snow), `city` (windows), `clouds` and `fog`, plus our\n1407\t  painter layers: `mesa`, `cliffs`, `village` (terraced houses), `balloons` (drifting), `lanterns` (rising), `aurora`\n1408\t  (animated ribbons), `stars` (with shooting stars), `skyline-neon` (blinking) and `rainbow` (sun shower).\n1409\t- **Count.** About 16 presets in all: one per stage, with the beach kit's dawn and sunset variants.\n1410\t\n1411\t### 9.3 Cars as shapes, pre-rendered\n1412\t\n1413\t- **Writing the shape.** Each car is a set of `E.SHAPES` entries in `src/02-shapes.js` (`ob-car-orchidea-under`, `-over`, `-lights`), written facing south per the asset card\n1414\t  (front +y, so *rear* is −y).\n1415\t  - **Parts:** body side profile as `prism` (outline on x/z, pushed along y), bonnet and boot as `wedge`/`prism`, wheels\n1416\t    as `cyl` rotated onto the x axis, seats as `box`, roll hoops as `lathe`/`cyl`, lamps as `box` in glow materials.\n1417\t  - **Materials:** the slot `paint` uses a key ramp of five tones, so recolouring is a pure palette swap. Also `chrome`\n1418\t    (`steel`), `glass` and `tyre`.\n1419\t- **Three shapes per car:** `…-under` (floor, seats, dash, wheel), `…-over` (doors, rear deck, bumpers, lamps' housings,\n1420\t  wheels) and `…-lights` (tail, brake and head lamps only, glow material).\n1421\t- **Pre-rendering.** At boot, render each with `E.shapeSprite(id, { view: OB.CHASE_VIEW, facing, size })`. The view is\n1422\t  `new E.View('chase', 'Chase', 0, 9)`, a camera looking north. The facing is `−π/2 + yaw`. That gives 33 yaws (−90° to\n1423\t  +90°, 5.625° apart) and 32 spin frames.\n1424\t  - **Check it** with `node engine/tools/asset-sheet.mjs src/02-shapes.js#ob-car-orchidea-over`: the sheet shows every view\n1425\t    and facing, and its lint flags thin parts.\n1426\t- **Seat anchors** per yaw are computed by projecting the seat points through the same view and stored with the frames.\n1427\t- **Traffic.** Ten traffic shapes, rendered at 9 yaws (−40° to +40°) × 2 layers (body and lights).\n1428\t- **Headless tests skip all pre-rendering.** `OB.Art` hands out stand-in frames with the right `w`/`h` and anchors, so\n1429\t  the sim and its tests run in about a second.\n1430\t\n1431\t### 9.4 Scenery and other sprites\n1432\t\n1433\t- **Core props:** `E.prop(name).frames[0]` for palm, tree, pine, bush, flowers, rock, cactus, streetlamp, sign, fence,\n1434\t  lamp, awning, lantern and barrel. Tint them with `color` where the kit needs it.\n1435\t- **Store shapes.** `kenney-nature` gives cliffs, trees and rocks, and `kenney-fantasy-town` houses for villages. Cut\n1436\t  only the ids used into `src/assets-ob.js` with `engine/tools/asset-set.mjs`. Load it with `E.addAssets` and credit\n1437\t  the sets on the About screen.\n1438\t- **New sprites** (★ in §8.6) are either `E.SHAPES` entries rendered at 2–3 facings with `OB.CHASE_VIEW`, or string\n1439\t  sprites (`E.sprite`) for the small things: balloons, bees, cones, bouquets, petals, glyph icons.\n1440\t- **Animated scenery** uses `E.anim`: turbines, waterfalls, flags, lanterns, neon, aurora, flamingos.\n1441\t- **Landmarks** (the Ferris wheel, cathedral, casino flower, castle, lift bridge, radio dish, lighthouse) are shapes or\n1442\t  custom `draw(g, sx, sy, scale)` objects built with `px.poly`/`px.rect` and toned with `E.tones`.\n1443\t\n1444\t### 9.5 UI art\n1445\t\n1446\t- **Logo.** `E.font.title` at scale 5, with `colors: ['#fff4e0', '#ffb3d1', '#ff7a59', '#ffcf4a']`, depth 4 and\n1447\t  `depthColor: '#5a1e4a'`. A sunflower replaces the \"O\", and a white shine sweeps across every 6 s.\n1448\t- **Panels.** `E.ui.box` with `bg: ['#2a1450', '#5a2a6a']` (dusk), a border in `#ffd6a0`, and accents in pink\n1449\t  `#ff5fa2`, sun `#ffcf4a` and sea `#3ac8e0`.\n1450\t- **Glyph icons.** 12×12 sprites, drawn at scale 2 for prompts (Appendix C).\n1451\t- **Postcards and garden beds** are drawn panels with a stamp, a portrait and an icon. Every icon gets a shape or\n1452\t  letter as well as its colour.\n1453\t\n1454\t---\n1455\t\n1456\t## 10. Build order and milestones\n1457\t\n1458\tBuild in this order. Each milestone ends with `node tests/run.mjs` green and a fresh bundle that opens from `file://`.\n1459\tEarlier milestones may use plain art, but never stubs. Anything built must work fully. Polish comes later, and nothing\n1460\tis cut.\n1461\t\n1462\t| Milestone | Build | Done when |\n1463\t|---|---|---|\n1464\t| **M0 Foundations** | the dev folder, the handoff engine copied, Playwright installed; `src/manifest.json`, `index.html`, `build.mjs` with the session client inlined first in `<head>`; `tests/run.mjs` printing only failures and a one-line count; `00-core.js` (bus, rand, tuning), `90-main.js` (game, boot steps, a real title scene with the logo); `game.debug` | the bundle opens with no errors (browser smoke test); the headless boot reaches `title` |\n1465\t| **M1 Road and feel** (the riskiest first) | E1/E2 road junctions and dressing; S1, S2 and S3 with real layouts and first-pass scenery; `CarSim` complete (§7.3); traffic (§7.4); the clock and checkpoints; the HUD core; the Orchidea model pre-rendered (layers and yaws); `OB.Brain` bot; the dev panel's **Tuning** tab and debug overlay | the physics suite passes (curve-hold targets, drift entry and exit, slipstream, bumps, spins, rails); the junction suite shows a seamless switch (≤ 1 px); the bot drives S1→S2 and S1→S3 inside the target times; a filmstrip of a drift through a 5-curve looks right |\n1466\t| **M2 The Arcade loop** | all 15 stages and 5 goals as real data with the kit rules; the route graph; scoring and the Bloom chain; results and initials; first-pass ending vignettes; title → menu → garage → radio → race; the UI kit with glyph prompts; settings (§7.20) with E5; the profile save; the synth extension E6/E7 with 2 radio songs, the engine note and core sound effects; E3 analog input | the 16-route playthrough passes (the bot reaches every goal in time on Normal); UI navigation passes for pad, keys and mouse; the glyph suite passes; content lint passes for the stages |\n1467\t| **M3 Platform features** | AI Hub ghost session with our card (§7.12); attract mode (§7.22); touch-only (§7.21); every Dev Mode tab (§7.23) in the pause menu; local Versus split screen (§7.11); E4, E8 | the fake-hub suite and the **harness in a browser** pass (`__hubCheck.pass`, 2 and 4 players, latency and loss); the attract suite passes; touch emulation shows the notice; the dev suite passes (slider changes behaviour, reset restores it); two Versus bots finish |\n1468\t| **M4 The passenger** | the cast's rigs in the car with layers and anchors; portraits; speech bubbles; the babble voice; Love in Bloom with all 14 request types and their objects; petals, grades, fork preferences; every ending (5 × 3) with all 45 passenger lines | the request suite has a pass and a fail script per type; the grade maths passes; a Love in Bloom playthrough reaches each mood; babble planner tests pass |\n1469\t| **M5 Career and meta** | rival personas; all 14 missions and their rules; the Tour map, briefs and debriefs; the garden and economy; Time Trial with ghosts and medals; records | every mission is cleared by its bot at C or better and AAA is possible with cheats off; the economy suite passes; ghosts survive record → save → load → replay |\n1470\t| **M6 Audio content** | all 7 radio songs, 4 themes, every jingle; the full sound-effect list (§7.15); per-car engine notes; ambience per kit; radio switching; ducking; tunnel reverb | audio lint (levels, clipping, silence) and the spectrum bands pass for every song; every sound-effect name in the data exists |\n1471\t| **M7 Art and effects** | all 13 kits fully dressed; every landmark; all skies; bloom, flare, shimmer and weather (sun shower, petal storm, aurora, fireworks, shooting stars); every car, traffic vehicle, rig and request-object animation (§7.13) | the screenshot review of each stage at 1920×1080 is done and noted in the log; `tools/check.mjs` look notes are acted on; there are no flat empty roadsides |\n1472\t| **M8 UX polish and balance** | transitions; hints; How to Play pages with live demos; About & Credits; comfort settings; HUD size; balance with the sliders and bot metrics (§11) | the readability suite passes (no text below scale 1, ≤ 7 items per screen); the balance windows hold (§11, R2) |\n1473\t| **M9 Release** | the full test pass; the final bundle; `exit-interview.json` (parsed); the About pages and the chat reply with the real Source Information and feedback; `engine/CHANGES.md` complete | everything in §13 is ticked |\n1474\t\n1475\t**Work habits.**\n1476\t- Rerun only the suite that covers what you changed (`node tests/run.mjs --only physics`), and the full command before\n1477\t  ending a milestone.\n1478\t- Write nothing outside the dev folder and the root deliverables (the HTML and `exit-interview.json`).\n1479\t- Note every engine change in `engine/CHANGES.md` as you make it."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6eff3345-97e6-4e65-b411-5b68a8aed193","uuid":"d31368ea-4b56-4d65-8d10-b9b2d14b5c3f","timestamp":"2026-11-04T10:52:14.753Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build__20261104-035148__xe1b7e1/work/PLAN.md","content":"| `truck` | flower lorry | 65–85 | 0.30 | potted plants; 2 lengths |\n| `tractor` | tractor | 35–50 | 0.24 | farm stages only; blinking orange light |\n| `scooter` | scooter | 80–100 | 0.10 | rider rig |\n| `icecream` | ice-cream truck | 60–75 | 0.24 | plays its jingle within 3,000 units |\n\nDensities by stage come from §8.2's mix weights × the base density 0.32 (vehicles per 10,000 units per lane). Hard\nstages add 0.05.\n\n**Roadside sets per kit.** These are sprites. Core props are `E.prop(name)`, store and own shapes are `E.shapeSprite`,\nand ★ marks what we draw new.\n\n| Kit | Ground and strips | Scenery |\n|---|---|---|\n| beach | sand and water | palm, ★umbrella, ★beach hut, ★lifeguard tower, ★surf rack, rock, ★buoy, ★Ferris-wheel pier |\n| fields | `rows` in lavender or sunflower colours | ★cypress, bush, ★farmhouse, ★beehives, ★silo, ★barn, ★wind turbine (animated), ★hay bale, fence |\n| cliffs | cliff edge and rail | rock (store `kenney-nature` cliff pieces), ★lemon tree, ★terraced house cluster, ★chapel, sign |\n| lake | water and pines | pine, ★boathouse, ★paddle steamer (moving), ★truss bridge (strip and spans), ★waterfall (animated) |\n| canyon | rock and sand | ★mesa pillar, ★rock arch (span), cactus in flower (★ a variant of the core cactus), rock, ★souvenir stand |\n| alpine | snow and grass | ★cherry tree (blossom, petals), pine, ★chalet, ★cow, ★snow shed (span), rock |\n| town | cobble and facades | ★arcade facade (strip), awning, ★market stall, lantern, streetlamp, ★fountain, ★cathedral dome (landmark), banners (bunting span) |\n| vineyard | `rows` of grapes | ★cypress, ★villa, barrel, ★castle on a hill (landmark), ★wine cart, fence |\n| lagoon | water both sides, low rails | ★flamingo (flock animation), ★salt pyramid, ★lift bridge, lamp |\n| jungle | grass and wet | palm, ★giant leaf, ★fern, ★ruin arch (span), ★orchid (emissive), ★waterfall, ★parrot |\n| night city | facades and a neon strip | ★neon sign set (6 messages), ★yacht, ★casino flower (landmark), streetlamp (lit), ★funicular |\n| night desert | sand | cactus, ★night-bloom cactus (emissive), ★motel sign, ★radio dish, rock |\n| highlands | heather and stone walls | ★standing stone, ★castle ruin, rock, bush (heather tint), ★sheep |\n\n**Request objects** (★ all): heart balloon (3 colours), gnome (googly eyes), bee swarm (Blobs with `wings: true`), ribbon\n(a pink strip between 2 cars), cone, flower arch, bouquet, photo box (asphalt paint) with a photographer rig, gate\nflowers in 5 colours (Memory Lane).\n\n**Signs** (★): the fork sign (two names, arrows, flowers), CHECKPOINT and GOAL arches, ROAD CLOSED barriers, the\nwind-sock warning, the stage name boards, and billboards with original fake ads: \"Gelato Gelatissimo\", \"Fioretti — Born\nto Bloom\", \"Radio Petal FM\", \"Hotel Vespera\". Billboards are written in the pixel font.\n\n### 8.7 Love in Bloom requests (all 14)\n\nThresholds are scaled by the `requestDifficulty` tuning. A miss gives 0 petals and a mood drop.\n\n| Type | Call-out (card) | Setup in the window | Measure | Perfect / Great / Good |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `pass` | PASS {n} CARS! | traffic ×1.6; n is 6–10 | clean overtakes | ≥ n / ≥ .75n / ≥ .5n |\n| `drift` | DRIFT FOR ME! | the window covers the stage's twistiest part | drift score against target T (2 long drifts) | ≥ T / ≥ .7T / ≥ .4T |\n| `ribbons` | CUT THE RIBBONS! | k = 4–6 ribbon pairs | snapped / k | 100 % / ≥ 70 % / ≥ 40 % |\n| `balloons` | POP THE BALLOONS! | 10–16 balloons at car height in lines and zigzags | popped / total | ≥ 90 % / ≥ 70 % / ≥ 45 % |\n| `gnomes` | BONK THE GNOMES! | 8–12 gnomes standing on the road | bonked / total | ≥ 90 % / ≥ 70 % / ≥ 45 % |\n| `bees` | DODGE THE BEES! | 6–10 swarms crossing lanes on sine paths | hits (a hit stings: a wobble and a mood drop) | 0 / 1 / 2 |\n| `cones` | SLALOM THE CONES! | 8–12 cones on a line | cones passed on the correct alternating side | ≥ 90 % / ≥ 70 % / ≥ 45 % |\n| `arches` | THROUGH THE ARCHES! | 6–8 one-lane flower arches that step between lanes | passed / total | 100 % / ≥ 75 % / ≥ 50 % |\n| `speed` | KEEP IT OVER {v}! | v is 230–260 (in the player's units) | share of the window above v | ≥ 95 % / ≥ 80 % / ≥ 60 % |\n| `clean` | NOT A SCRATCH! | a window of about 15 s with dense traffic | contacts (bumps, crashes, scrapes) | 0 / — / 1 |\n| `draft` | STAY IN THE DRAFT! | traffic queues to draft behind | seconds of slipstream above 0.5 | ≥ 6 / ≥ 4 / ≥ 2 |\n| `close` | CLOSE CALLS! | denser traffic | near misses | ≥ n / ≥ .7n / ≥ .4n (n is 5–6) |\n| `bouquets` | GRAB THE BOUQUETS! | 10–14 bouquets, some on the inside of bends and beside traffic | collected / total | ≥ 90 % / ≥ 70 % / ≥ 45 % |\n| `photo` | STOP FOR A PHOTO! | a painted box, 2 segments long, at a landmark, with a photographer | **Perfect:** below 50 km/h with the car wholly inside the box and under 3.0 s lost. **Great:** stopped in the box. **Good:** through the box below 100 km/h. | — |\n\nThe photo stop costs clock time, a deliberate risk against the reward. Juni and Vivi say a different line for it.\n\n### 8.8 The Coast 2 Coast Tour missions (14)\n\n| # | Giver · title | Route | Rules (`OB.Rules` kind) | C / B / A / AAA | Seeds (base) and unlock |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| B1 | Bruno · **First Light Sprint** | S1 to its checkpoint | `sprint`: reach it with the clock from 52 s | 0 s / 5 s / 9 s / 13 s left | 150 |\n| B2 | Bruno · **Drift Duel** | S2 | `driftduel` against Kiki: more drift points by the stage's end. Live points bar for both. | win / +10 % / +25 % / +45 % margin | 200 · the Gardenia bed |\n| B3 | Bruno · **Draft Dash** | S4 | `draftdash` against the Bramble Brothers: slipstream points (seconds × speed) plus finishing first | win / +10 % / +25 % / +40 % | 250 |\n| B4 | Bruno · **Last One Wilts** | S5 → S8 → S12, then Goal B | `knockout`: 5 racers (you, Kiki, Brambles, Lustres, Ottavio). The last at each checkpoint is out; finish first. | top 2 / 1st / 1st +2 s / 1st +5 s | 300 · the \"Lemon Drop Rush\" bed |\n| M1 | Marisol · **Balloon Bonanza** | S1 | `collect` balloons: 40 spread over the stage | 20 / 26 / 32 / 38 | 150 |\n| M2 | Marisol · **Gnome Patrol** | S9 | `collect` gnomes (24) with bees: a bee hit costs 2 | 10 / 14 / 18 / 22 net | 200 · the Picnic Gingham outfit |\n| M3 | Marisol · **Memory Lane** | S2 | `memory`: she shows a 5-colour flower sequence (2 s per colour), then 5 gate sets with 3 coloured gates each; drive through the right colour | 3 / 4 / 5 / 5 with no hints shown | 250 · the \"Moonflower\" bed |\n| V1 | Vivi · **Not a Scratch** | S8 | `clean`: zero contacts, ranked by time | under par +12 s / +6 s / +2 s / under par | 200 |\n| V2 | Vivi · **Fashionably Fast** | S10 | `speedkeep`: above 240 km/h for the stage, in crosswinds | 70 % / 80 % / 88 % / 95 % | 250 · the Magnolia bed |\n| V3 | Vivi · **Heartbreakers** | S11 → Goal A | `heartbreak` against Lance and Lacey: both couples start with 20 petals. Every 2 s behind them drains 1, every pass steals 2 (both ways), and theirs drain the same. End with more than 0, or make theirs 0. | > 0 / ≥ 10 / ≥ 18 / theirs at 0 | 300 · the Gala Gown outfit |\n| J1 | Juni · **Close Calls** | S3 | `count` near misses | 8 / 12 / 15 / 20 | 150 |\n| J2 | Juni · **Ribbon Cutter** | S7 | `count` ribbons: 16 pairs | 8 / 11 / 14 / 16 | 200 · the Rally Suit outfit |\n| J3 | Juni · **The Perfect Shot** | S6 | `photo3`: 3 photo boxes; the score is the sum of photo ratings | 1 Good each / 2 Great / 3 Great / 3 Perfect | 250 · the Mirror Roads bed |\n| F | Bruno and all · **Coast 2 Coast** | the full pyramid, forks open | `love`: Love in Bloom rules with all request types, and the passenger you choose | grade B / A / AA / AAA at any goal | 500 · the Girasole bed, the credits |\n\nEvery rival mission shows the rival's portrait and a live gap on the HUD.\n\n### 8.9 The Garage Garden: beds and costs\n\n| Bed | Seeds | Bed | Seeds |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Gardenia GT Targa | 600 | Song \"Lemon Drop Rush\" | 300 |\n| Magnolia V12 Barchetta | 900 | Song \"Moonflower\" | 300 |\n| Girasole Hyper Spider | 1500 | Paint packs ×4 | 100 each |\n| Primavera Bloom Edition | 400 | Outfits ×3 | 150 each |\n| Orchidea Bloom Edition | 500 | Mirror Roads | 800 |\n\nThat totals **6,150 seeds**. All 14 missions at A (base 3,350 × 1.5) give 5,025. Arcade runs give about 150–350 each, so\nthe economy test's \"everything within 14 A-ranks plus 6 runs\" holds.\n\n### 8.10 Music (all original, all in code)\n\nRadio stations, in dial order:\n\n| # | Station | Song | BPM · key | Style and instruments | Progression (per 2 bars) | Hook idea |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | 88.6 PETAL FM | **Sunburst Samba** | 128 · D major | Latin fusion: congas, timbales, slap bass, brass stabs, steel-drum lead, e-piano comping | Dmaj7 · Bm7 · Em7 · A7sus4–A7 | the steel drum climbs a 6th and falls by step, with a timbale fill into each chorus |\n| 2 | 91.2 SUN FM | **Coastline Crush** | 116 · F major | Balearic pop-funk: four on the floor, a chorus-guitar skank, finger bass, a soft pulse lead, pad | Fmaj7 · Em7 · Dm7 · Gm7–C9 | a breezy 3-note pickup into a long held note |\n| 3 | 94.8 SHORE FM | **Shuffle on the Shore** | 110 shuffle (swing in 12/8 feel, steps 3) · B♭ major | jazz shuffle: organ, walking bass, brushed snare, sax lead | B♭6 · G7 · Cm7 · F7 (turnaround) | call and response between the sax and the organ |\n| 4 | 99.1 VESPA FM | **Vespa Vendetta** | 152 · A minor | Eurobeat: octave saw bass, supersaw stabs, fast 16th arps, a bright lead | Am · F · G · E7 | the chorus jumps an octave on beat 3 |\n| 5 | 103.5 PASTEL FM | **Pastel Highway** | 104 · E♭ major | city pop: DX-style e-piano, slap bass, sax or lead, claps on 2 and 4, pad | A♭maj7 · Gm7 · Fm7 · B♭7sus4 | a sax lick answered by e-piano chords |\n| 6 | 106.3 ZEST FM (unlock) | **Lemon Drop Rush** | 140 · G major | acid jazz into breakbeat: breakbeat drums, a rubbery 303-ish bass (resonant filter env), organ stabs | G7 · C7 · G7 · D7–C7 | an organ riff in 4ths |\n| 7 | 108.8 MOON FM (unlock) | **Moonflower** | 92 · C♯ minor | a night synth ballad: a pad, marimba arps, a soft lead, a slow kick | C♯m9 · Amaj7 · E · B | a long lead line with a slide |\n\nOther music:\n\n| Song | Use | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| **OutBloom!** | title | 120, C major; a brass fanfare into a samba groove |\n| **Garden Party** | menus, the Tour, the garden | 100 bossa nova; e-piano and nylon pluck |\n| **Petal Count** | results | 112; a light funk loop |\n| **Last Light Lullaby** | the credits and Bloom endings | 84 |\n\nJingles (played with `sting`):\n\n| Jingle | Feel |\n|---|---|\n| start fanfare | 2 bars |\n| checkpoint | a bright V–I |\n| request start | a rising triplet |\n| request success | a major arpeggio up |\n| request fail | the trombone slide |\n| fork tick | — |\n| Full Bloom | a sparkle sweep |\n| goal fanfare | 4 bars |\n| new record | — |\n| unlock bloom | — |\n| game over | \"Sunset Blues\", a slow minor-blues turnaround |\n\nEvery song needs its named layers and a `sparkle` layer (§7.14).\n\n### 8.11 Writing style and the line bank\n\n**Style.**\n- Short, sunny, cheeky, like a peppy arcade passenger who is half sweetheart, half backseat driver.\n- Exclamation-heavy but never mean. A touch of cheese and a wink. No profanity.\n- **Lengths:** call-outs ≤ 20 characters, in caps. Spoken lines ≤ 40 characters, 1–2 per bubble.\n- Banners are 1–3 words. Ending captions are a postcard sentence.\n- Each character's voice stays distinct: Marisol bubbly, Vivi theatrical (\"Darling…\"), Juni breathless slang, Bruno\n  booming with \"champ\".\n- **Required counts:** each passenger has ≥ 4 variants for each reaction (request intro per type, Perfect, Great, Good,\n  Miss, bump, crash, near miss, air, fork preference left and right, checkpoint, low time, goal, start). Bruno has ≥ 3\n  per event.\n\n**Seed lines** (use them, then write the rest in the same voice):\n\n| Who | Event | Lines |\n|---|---|---|\n| Marisol | start | \"Coast to coast? With you? Let's GO!\" |\n| Marisol | balloons | \"Ooh, balloons! Pop them all for me!\" |\n| Marisol | Perfect | \"Eee! You're amazing!\" · \"Again, again!\" |\n| Marisol | Miss | \"Aww… you missed so many.\" |\n| Marisol | crash | \"Kyaaa! My hair!\" |\n| Marisol | fork left | \"Left! I want to smell the lavender!\" |\n| Marisol | low time | \"Hurry, the sun's slipping!\" |\n| Vivi | start | \"Darling, do try not to dent anything.\" |\n| Vivi | speed | \"Faster. I refuse to arrive second.\" |\n| Vivi | Great | \"Hmm. Adequate. Barely.\" |\n| Vivi | Perfect | \"Now THAT is how one arrives!\" |\n| Vivi | bump | \"Was that a… car? Unacceptable.\" |\n| Vivi | fork right | \"Right, the cliffs. Everyone who matters goes that way.\" |\n| Juni | start | \"Okay okay okay, get me some ACTION!\" |\n| Juni | close | \"Closer! Closer! I need the shot!\" |\n| Juni | near miss | \"WHOA, did you see that?!\" |\n| Juni | Miss | \"Boooring. Give me danger!\" |\n| Juni | Perfect | \"Best shot of my LIFE!\" |\n| Juni | air | \"We're FLYING!\" |\n| Bruno | start | \"Morning, champ! The west coast is waiting!\" |\n| Bruno | drift | \"Ho ho! You drift like my rally days!\" |\n| Bruno | mission win | \"Mamma mia, what a finish!\" |\n| Bruno | mission fail | \"Again, champ! Even I spun out in '79.\" |\n| Announcer | — | \"CHECKPOINT!\" · \"TIME EXTENDED!\" · \"GOAL!\" · \"FULL BLOOM!\" · \"PERFECT START!\" · \"NEW RECORD!\" |\n\nEnding captions (one per goal and mood), for example: \"GOAL A · LANTERN PIER: A thousand lanterns, and one just for\nyou two.\" · \"GOAL E · FINISTERRA LIGHTHOUSE: The green flash. Best. Day. Ever.\"\n\nHelp, briefs and the How to Play text use the same voice, plainly.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Art direction and asset recipes\n\n### 9.1 Rules\n\n- **Base style.** The engine's native style: pixel art at 480×270, shaded with `E.tones` (cool shadows, warm lights, a\n  `hi` sparkle). Everything is drawn with `E.px.*`, `E.font`, sprites and shapes. Never use `ctx.arc`, `stroke`,\n  `fillText` or canvas gradients on the buffer. Translucency goes through `px.blend` or the dither helpers.\n- **Fog and distance.** Use the road module's fog (colour per time of day). Far sprites fade to the fog colour at the\n  module's stepped alpha.\n- **Palettes.** Every kit has five swatches: `ground`, `ground2`, `accent`, `rock` and `sky` ramps. Time of day applies\n  a grade on top: dawn peach and lilac, noon clean and saturated, late afternoon gold, sunset magenta to orange, night\n  indigo with neon.\n- **Sizes.**\n  - **The player's car** is about 128 px wide at the screen's bottom (480 wide).\n  - **Traffic** is drawn from the same model scale.\n  - **Palms** at the edge reach about 160 px tall near the camera.\n  - **Rigs in the car:** heads about 12–14 px, with the upper body showing.\n  - **Ending rigs** are about 70 px tall (size 1.6).\n\n### 9.2 Sky presets (`OB.Sky`, in `01-data-world.js`)\n\nEach sky is `{ gradient: [5 colours], sun | moon: { x, y, r, color, halo }, clouds: { kind, color, y, speed },\nlayers: [...] }`.\n\n- **Layer kinds:** the engine's `sea`, `hills`, `mountains` (snow), `city` (windows), `clouds` and `fog`, plus our\n  painter layers: `mesa`, `cliffs`, `village` (terraced houses), `balloons` (drifting), `lanterns` (rising), `aurora`\n  (animated ribbons), `stars` (with shooting stars), `skyline-neon` (blinking) and `rainbow` (sun shower).\n- **Count.** About 16 presets in all: one per stage, with the beach kit's dawn and sunset variants.\n\n### 9.3 Cars as shapes, pre-rendered\n\n- **Writing the shape.** Each car is a set of `E.SHAPES` entries in `src/02-shapes.js` (`ob-car-orchidea-under`, `-over`, `-lights`), written facing south per the asset card\n  (front +y, so *rear* is −y).\n  - **Parts:** body side profile as `prism` (outline on x/z, pushed along y), bonnet and boot as `wedge`/`prism`, wheels\n    as `cyl` rotated onto the x axis, seats as `box`, roll hoops as `lathe`/`cyl`, lamps as `box` in glow materials.\n  - **Materials:** the slot `paint` uses a key ramp of five tones, so recolouring is a pure palette swap. Also `chrome`\n    (`steel`), `glass` and `tyre`.\n- **Three shapes per car:** `…-under` (floor, seats, dash, wheel), `…-over` (doors, rear deck, bumpers, lamps' housings,\n  wheels) and `…-lights` (tail, brake and head lamps only, glow material).\n- **Pre-rendering.** At boot, render each with `E.shapeSprite(id, { view: OB.CHASE_VIEW, facing, size })`. The view is\n  `new E.View('chase', 'Chase', 0, 9)`, a camera looking north. The facing is `−π/2 + yaw`. That gives 33 yaws (−90° to\n  +90°, 5.625° apart) and 32 spin frames.\n  - **Check it** with `node engine/tools/asset-sheet.mjs src/02-shapes.js#ob-car-orchidea-over`: the sheet shows every view\n    and facing, and its lint flags thin parts.\n- **Seat anchors** per yaw are computed by projecting the seat points through the same view and stored with the frames.\n- **Traffic.** Ten traffic shapes, rendered at 9 yaws (−40° to +40°) × 2 layers (body and lights).\n- **Headless tests skip all pre-rendering.** `OB.Art` hands out stand-in frames with the right `w`/`h` and anchors, so\n  the sim and its tests run in about a second.\n\n### 9.4 Scenery and other sprites\n\n- **Core props:** `E.prop(name).frames[0]` for palm, tree, pine, bush, flowers, rock, cactus, streetlamp, sign, fence,\n  lamp, awning, lantern and barrel. Tint them with `color` where the kit needs it.\n- **Store shapes.** `kenney-nature` gives cliffs, trees and rocks, and `kenney-fantasy-town` houses for villages. Cut\n  only the ids used into `src/assets-ob.js` with `engine/tools/asset-set.mjs`. Load it with `E.addAssets` and credit\n  the sets on the About screen.\n- **New sprites** (★ in §8.6) are either `E.SHAPES` entries rendered at 2–3 facings with `OB.CHASE_VIEW`, or string\n  sprites (`E.sprite`) for the small things: balloons, bees, cones, bouquets, petals, glyph icons.\n- **Animated scenery** uses `E.anim`: turbines, waterfalls, flags, lanterns, neon, aurora, flamingos.\n- **Landmarks** (the Ferris wheel, cathedral, casino flower, castle, lift bridge, radio dish, lighthouse) are shapes or\n  custom `draw(g, sx, sy, scale)` objects built with `px.poly`/`px.rect` and toned with `E.tones`.\n\n### 9.5 UI art\n\n- **Logo.** `E.font.title` at scale 5, with `colors: ['#fff4e0', '#ffb3d1', '#ff7a59', '#ffcf4a']`, depth 4 and\n  `depthColor: '#5a1e4a'`. A sunflower replaces the \"O\", and a white shine sweeps across every 6 s.\n- **Panels.** `E.ui.box` with `bg: ['#2a1450', '#5a2a6a']` (dusk), a border in `#ffd6a0`, and accents in pink\n  `#ff5fa2`, sun `#ffcf4a` and sea `#3ac8e0`.\n- **Glyph icons.** 12×12 sprites, drawn at scale 2 for prompts (Appendix C).\n- **Postcards and garden beds** are drawn panels with a stamp, a portrait and an icon. Every icon gets a shape or\n  letter as well as its colour.\n\n---\n\n## 10. Build order and milestones\n\nBuild in this order. Each milestone ends with `node tests/run.mjs` green and a fresh bundle that opens from `file://`.\nEarlier milestones may use plain art, but never stubs. Anything built must work fully. Polish comes later, and nothing\nis cut.\n\n| Milestone | Build | Done when |\n|---|---|---|\n| **M0 Foundations** | the dev folder, the handoff engine copied, Playwright installed; `src/manifest.json`, `index.html`, `build.mjs` with the session client inlined first in `<head>`; `tests/run.mjs` printing only failures and a one-line count; `00-core.js` (bus, rand, tuning), `90-main.js` (game, boot steps, a real title scene with the logo); `game.debug` | the bundle opens with no errors (browser smoke test); the headless boot reaches `title` |\n| **M1 Road and feel** (the riskiest first) | E1/E2 road junctions and dressing; S1, S2 and S3 with real layouts and first-pass scenery; `CarSim` complete (§7.3); traffic (§7.4); the clock and checkpoints; the HUD core; the Orchidea model pre-rendered (layers and yaws); `OB.Brain` bot; the dev panel's **Tuning** tab and debug overlay | the physics suite passes (curve-hold targets, drift entry and exit, slipstream, bumps, spins, rails); the junction suite shows a seamless switch (≤ 1 px); the bot drives S1→S2 and S1→S3 inside the target times; a filmstrip of a drift through a 5-curve looks right |\n| **M2 The Arcade loop** | all 15 stages and 5 goals as real data with the kit rules; the route graph; scoring and the Bloom chain; results and initials; first-pass ending vignettes; title → menu → garage → radio → race; the UI kit with glyph prompts; settings (§7.20) with E5; the profile save; the synth extension E6/E7 with 2 radio songs, the engine note and core sound effects; E3 analog input | the 16-route playthrough passes (the bot reaches every goal in time on Normal); UI navigation passes for pad, keys and mouse; the glyph suite passes; content lint passes for the stages |\n| **M3 Platform features** | AI Hub ghost session with our card (§7.12); attract mode (§7.22); touch-only (§7.21); every Dev Mode tab (§7.23) in the pause menu; local Versus split screen (§7.11); E4, E8 | the fake-hub suite and the **harness in a browser** pass (`__hubCheck.pass`, 2 and 4 players, latency and loss); the attract suite passes; touch emulation shows the notice; the dev suite passes (slider changes behaviour, reset restores it); two Versus bots finish |\n| **M4 The passenger** | the cast's rigs in the car with layers and anchors; portraits; speech bubbles; the babble voice; Love in Bloom with all 14 request types and their objects; petals, grades, fork preferences; every ending (5 × 3) with all 45 passenger lines | the request suite has a pass and a fail script per type; the grade maths passes; a Love in Bloom playthrough reaches each mood; babble planner tests pass |\n| **M5 Career and meta** | rival personas; all 14 missions and their rules; the Tour map, briefs and debriefs; the garden and economy; Time Trial with ghosts and medals; records | every mission is cleared by its bot at C or better and AAA is possible with cheats off; the economy suite passes; ghosts survive record → save → load → replay |\n| **M6 Audio content** | all 7 radio songs, 4 themes, every jingle; the full sound-effect list (§7.15); per-car engine notes; ambience per kit; radio switching; ducking; tunnel reverb | audio lint (levels, clipping, silence) and the spectrum bands pass for every song; every sound-effect name in the data exists |\n| **M7 Art and effects** | all 13 kits fully dressed; every landmark; all skies; bloom, flare, shimmer and weather (sun shower, petal storm, aurora, fireworks, shooting stars); every car, traffic vehicle, rig and request-object animation (§7.13) | the screenshot review of each stage at 1920×1080 is done and noted in the log; `tools/check.mjs` look notes are acted on; there are no flat empty roadsides |\n| **M8 UX polish and balance** | transitions; hints; How to Play pages with live demos; About & Credits; comfort settings; HUD size; balance with the sliders and bot metrics (§11) | the readability suite passes (no text below scale 1, ≤ 7 items per screen); the balance windows hold (§11, R2) |\n| **M9 Release** | the full test pass; the final bundle; `exit-interview.json` (parsed); the About pages and the chat reply with the real Source Information and feedback; `engine/CHANGES.md` complete | everything in §13 is ticked |\n\n**Work habits.**\n- Rerun only the suite that covers what you changed (`node tests/run.mjs --only physics`), and the full command before\n  ending a milestone.\n- Write nothing outside the dev folder and the root deliverables (the HTML and `exit-interview.json`).\n- Note every engine change in `engine/CHANGES.md` as you make it.","numLines":280,"startLine":1200,"totalLines":1758}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01TpJQZngMwRUCR62pMTSqLV","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cghm84NiC7QjJ7FvbEajK","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_013jb3jHZrQd1zA23NrbLtSP","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build__20261104-035148__xe1b7e1/work/PLAN.md","offset":1480,"limit":280},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11821,"cache_read_input_tokens":67091,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11821},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6eff3345-97e6-4e65-b411-5b68a8aed193","uuid":"d2d7ae53-9675-4857-ad19-61c6740bc2e6","timestamp":"2026-11-04T10:52:18.554Z","request_id":"req_011Cghm848L57UfZCo7CKxro","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_013jb3jHZrQd1zA23NrbLtSP":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build__20261104-035148__xe1b7e1/work/PLAN.md","offset":1480,"limit":280}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013jb3jHZrQd1zA23NrbLtSP","type":"tool_result","content":"1480\t\n1481\t---\n1482\t\n1483\t## 11. Risks and how to de-risk them\n1484\t\n1485\t| # | Risk | Mitigation |\n1486\t|---|---|---|\n1487\t| R1 | **The fork renderer and the seamless stage switch.** The road module draws one road, so junctions are new code in the hottest path. | Build it first (M1). Keep it simple with the flat-junction rule (§7.1), one shared height profile and per-branch lateral offsets. Unit-test the projection: both branch centres; the road centre and player's screen x before and after the switch within 1 px; the gore cushion pushes to the nearer side. Record a filmstrip of a fork at 250 km/h, and review frames 1–16 for pops. |\n1488\t| R2 | **Drift and handling feel**, the heart of the game, tuned without hands on a pad. | Every constant is a live slider from M1. The physics suite asserts the design targets (§7.3: a 3 holdable flat out, a 4 needing a lift, a 6 drifted at about 85 %). **Balance windows**, asserted by the bot suite: on Normal, the reference bot finishes the all-left route with 15–30 s spare and the all-right route with 2–12 s. A \"sloppy\" bot (1 spin per stage) fails at least half the routes. A \"no-drift\" bot finishes no more than 6 of the 16. |\n1489\t| R3 | **Car sprites from shapes might look crude, or boot slowly.** | Prototype the Orchidea in M1 and check it with `asset-sheet.mjs` and in-race screenshots. If the shape renderer can't give clean curves at 128 px, draw the body with `px.poly` profiles per yaw band (the ROAD kit's method, 33 frames), which is just as complete. If boot exceeds about 8 s, render the cars a race needs behind a short \"Polishing the chrome…\" boot step at race start. |\n1490\t| R4 | **AI Hub conformance**, which is a hard gate. | Integrate in M3 using the ghost example's pattern. `ready()` never waits for input, and the client stays verbatim. Run the official harness in Playwright with 2 and 4 players, `latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5`, and the `host-leave` scenario. Re-run it on the final bundle (M9). |\n1491\t| R5 | **Audio quality, judged by builders who can't hear.** | Composition rules (§7.14), named instrument presets, and the offline render lint: in Playwright, `OfflineAudioContext` renders 12 s of each song through the real synth code. It asserts a peak under 0.98, RMS between 0.06 and 0.25, no silence over 1 s (except a designed rest), and band energy order (low < 250 Hz present, 1–4 kHz present). It also writes spectrogram PNGs to `.cache/audio/` to look at. |\n1492\t| R6 | **Content volume** (15 stages, 13 kits, 45 ending lines, 14 missions, 11 songs) causing gaps or placeholders. | Data schemas plus the **content-lint suite**: every stage has its fields, slots, landmarks and par; every goal has 3 moods × 3 passengers; every line key used exists and none is empty; every mission has rules, rewards and a bot; every bed has a cost and a reward source; bundle text has no TODO, placeholder or lorem. M2 lands all stages as real data early. |\n1493\t| R7 | **10-foot readability.** | UI-kit rules: a minimum scale and an item count, enforced in a debug-build assertion that the readability suite runs headless. Screenshots at 1920×1080 and 1280×720 reviewed in M7 and M8. The HUD's Extra-large size is tested too. |\n1494\t| R8 | **Headless speed and determinism.** | No pre-rendering headless. All gameplay randomness comes from seeded streams. `game.stateHash` covers the race state. A proof hash of 1,200 scripted steps run twice must match. |\n1495\t| R9 | **Split-screen layout.** | A per-scene `res` of `[640, 360]`, the rows layout, a HUD scaled for 180-px-tall viewports. Two-bot Versus test and screenshots. |\n1496\t| R10 | **Single-file bundle under `file://`.** | `build.mjs` lint (§4.3). The Playwright suite opens the root HTML by `file://`: no request leaves the page, there are no console errors, and the title is reached. |\n1497\t| R11 | **Touch-only false positives** (touchscreen laptops). | Require no fine pointer plus no keydown and no pad. Any key or pad clears the notice at once. Tested with emulation both ways. |\n1498\t| R12 | **Scope.** | Everything in this plan is in scope, and nothing more. Prefer finishing a system completely before starting the next, in milestone order. |\n1499\t\n1500\t---\n1501\t\n1502\t## 12. Testing\n1503\t\n1504\t### 12.1 The one command\n1505\t\n1506\t`node tests/run.mjs` runs everything from the dev folder:\n1507\t- the headless suites first (in about 10 s), then the browser suites;\n1508\t- `--quick` skips the browser suites, `--only <suite>[,<suite>]` runs only those, and `--update-par` rewrites the\n1509\t  stages' par and medal times from the reference bot.\n1510\t\n1511\t**Output.** One line per failure (`FAIL physics/curve-4-needs-lift: held 1.18 at 96% vtop`), then one summary line:\n1512\t`212 passed, 0 failed, 0 skipped (14.2 s)`. It exits with code 1 on any failure and prints nothing else.\n1513\t\n1514\t**The harness** (`tests/lib/boot.mjs`), as `game/test.mjs` does it:\n1515\t- Make a `vm` context with `window` (the context itself), `addEventListener`, `location` and `console` stand-ins.\n1516\t- Load `engine/my-3d2dge.js` and every file in `src/manifest.json`. The headless `E.Game` skips drawing.\n1517\t- Return `{ E, game, OB, I: game.input, snap: () => game.debug.snapshot(), events }`.\n1518\t- **Inputs:** `I.tap`, `I.hold`, `I.setAnalog` (E3), `I.feed` for the mouse. **Stepping:** `game.advance(s)`.\n1519\t- **Fresh state:** each suite boots a fresh context, so suites never share state.\n1520\t\n1521\t### 12.2 Headless suites\n1522\t\n1523\t| Suite | What it proves |\n1524\t|---|---|\n1525\t| `core` | boot reaches `title`; no `game.errors`; the bus delivers in order; tuning reset restores every default; rand streams repeat for a seed |\n1526\t| `road` | every stage builds within its length bounds; the first 150 segments and the junctions are flat; `info()` reports ground, edges and spans as the data says; the scenery is placed the same twice for one seed |\n1527\t| `junction` | branch centres at the decision point; the side chosen equals `sign(x)`; **seamless switch**: before and after it, the road centre's screen x is within 1 px and the player's z continues; gore cushion hits push to the nearer branch; a closed fork on a fixed route blocks that side |\n1528\t| `physics` | per-car top speed and 0–100 within 3 %; brakes; the curve-hold design targets; drift entry by all three inputs and none below 120 km/h; counter-steer straightens; drift score accumulates; the slipstream builds and the slingshot fires; off-road slowdown; rails scrape without spinning; bump against spin by closing speed; crest air and steering cut; gusts push |\n1529\t| `traffic` | the mix and density per stage; the minimum spacing; a two-lane gap always exists; lane changes avoid overlaps; junction branch assignment; ribbon pairs stay paired; overtake and near-miss detection with exact geometry cases; the horn makes the car ahead change lane |\n1530\t| `race` | the clock, checkpoint bonuses, difficulty scaling; time up → `gameover`; goal → `ending` → `results`; score maths; Bloom chain levels, decay and crash halving; the route string is recorded |\n1531\t| `playthrough-arcade` | **the bot drives all 16 routes to all 5 goals** on Normal within the R2 balance windows; no errors; every stage enters and every checkpoint fires once |\n1532\t| `requests` | **for each of the 14 types**, a scripted *pass* run (the bot targets the objects) rates Perfect, and a scripted *fail* run (drive straight in lane 1, or crash on purpose) rates Miss; petals and the favourite ×1.2; fork preference ±; penalties |\n1533\t| `playthrough-love` | Love in Bloom on 3 routes with 3 passengers reaches Bloom, Bud and Wilt (by scripted quality); every ending vignette runs to its end with every line found |\n1534\t| `missions` | **every mission** is cleared by its bot at C or better, and AAA is reachable with the \"perfect\" bot; the failure path works for each; rewards are paid once per rank improvement; unlock chains open the right postcards; the Finale opens at 9 cleared |\n1535\t| `economy` | 14 missions at A plus 6 arcade runs buy every bed; buying with too few seeds is refused; unlocks take effect in the garage and radio |\n1536\t| `trial` | a ghost records, saves, loads and replays to the same positions; splits; medals from par |\n1537\t| `versus` | two players join (pad and keyboard halves); a split-screen race with 2 bots finishes; places; rematch |\n1538\t| `hub` | with a **fake `AIHub`** in the context (`active: true`, `connect`, `ready`, `on`, `time`, `send`, `progress`, `result`, `answer`, `rng`), check: the title is skipped; `connect` uses ghost and `[1, 4]` with a card; `ready()` comes without input; Enter or ✕ on the card answers ready and △ answers more; the start syncs to `time() ≥ 0`; ghost sends happen at 8–12 Hz with `area`; progress comes once a second; the goal reports `result({ time })`; `time-up` reports `result({ score })` once; `end` stops input and sound; quit, restart and dev mode are hidden; Options with Create held doesn't pause. With `connect` resolving `null`, it boots to the title. |\n1539\t| `attract` | 10 s idle on each listed screen starts `attract`; 9.9 s doesn't; any key, pad, mouse move or click returns to the same screen and focus, and that input is consumed; a 100 s run emits fork, drift, request-Perfect, rival-or-versus and goal events; no profile writes |\n1540\t| `touch` | with stand-ins for touch-only media queries: the notice shows, the scenes have no touch buttons, and the attract mode runs; a tap doesn't end the attract mode; a keydown clears the notice |\n1541\t| `ui` | from the title, every screen in §5.3 is reachable with pad, keys and mouse (`I.feed`), and each has a way back; focus never lands on nothing; the readability assertion (text at scale 1 or more, at most 7 focusable items) holds on every screen |\n1542\t| `glyphs` | every action's prompt for keyboard, mouse, PlayStation and Xbox; the default is PlayStation; the setting switches to Xbox; rebinding changes the prompt; the device switch updates the prompts on the next step |\n1543\t| `dev` | the pause-menu Dev Mode item opens the panel; each slider changes its sim effect (for example, top speed changes v after 5 s of full gas); per-row and global reset; cheats work; every warp lands where it says; \"Keep after reload\" saves and restores |\n1544\t| `save` | the profile round-trip; a migration from a v0 fixture; blocked storage is survived; settings persist |\n1545\t| `voice` | the babble planner: syllable counts for fixtures, total duration in range, the same text giving the same plan, moods shifting pitch, punctuation pauses |\n1546\t| `audio-data` | every song parses (notes, chords, accents, slides); every track has an instrument; every song has a `sparkle` layer; every sound-effect and jingle name used exists |\n1547\t| `content` | the lint of R6: stages, goals, lines, missions, beds, songs, credits, `OB.ABOUT` fields all filled (no empty, \"TBD\" or placeholder strings) |\n1548\t| `determinism` | `proof()`: 1,200 scripted steps from a seed, hashed twice with the same result; the hash differs when the seed differs |\n1549\t\n1550\t### 12.3 Browser suites (Playwright, `tests/browser/`)\n1551\t\n1552\t| Suite | What it proves |\n1553\t|---|---|\n1554\t| `bundle` | `node build.mjs`, then open the **root HTML by `file://`** at 1920×1080: no console errors, no network requests, the title scene is reached, `game.debug.snapshot().scene === 'title'`; the lint of §4.3 |\n1555\t| `check` | `node engine/tools/check.mjs <root html>` exits 0; act on its look notes (and record them in the log) |\n1556\t| `hub-harness` | copy the bundle and `vendor/hub/*` into `.cache/hubtest/`; start `python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` in that folder; read the port it prints; confirm `/` serves our title; open `harness.html?game=<bundle>&players=2&auto=1`, then `players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1`, then `&scenario=host-leave`; each must reach `window.__hubCheck.pass === true`; kill the server by its PID |\n1557\t| `touch-emulation` | `check.mjs --size 390x844@3`: the notice is visible, no touch buttons are drawn, and the snapshot shows attract running after 11 s |\n1558\t| `audio-lint` | the offline render checks of R5 for every song and jingle |\n1559\t| `screens` | screenshots of title, menu, garage, radio, race (one per kit), a fork, Love in Bloom request, Tour map, garden, results, ending, Versus split, hub card and dev panel, at 1920×1080, into `.cache/shots/`, for the review in M7 and M8. This suite fails only on errors, never on looks. |\n1560\t\n1561\t### 12.4 What only eyes can check (screenshots and filmstrips)\n1562\t\n1563\tCheck these with `tools/filmstrip.mjs` or screenshots, and note them in the build log:\n1564\t- the drift and spin animation (filmstrip);\n1565\t- a fork at speed (filmstrip);\n1566\t- bloom and lens flare not smearing pixels;\n1567\t- night and tunnel tints;\n1568\t- the car layers and the occupants' alignment at every yaw (a contact sheet of 33 frames);\n1569\t- each kit's density and composition;\n1570\t- HUD legibility at 1280×720;\n1571\t- the hub card's look.\n1572\t\n1573\tEverything that can be a number, a state or an event is a script instead.\n1574\t\n1575\t---\n1576\t\n1577\t## 13. Definition of done: every TASK.md requirement and where it lives\n1578\t\n1579\t| TASK.md requirement | Where | Proved by |\n1580\t|---|---|---|\n1581\t| Genre and perspective that fit the source | §1, §7.2 | screens |\n1582\t| Title riffing on the name, picked by a real RNG | header | — |\n1583\t| Single HTML named per TASK.md; may use CDNs | §1 names, §4.3 (we need none) | `bundle` |\n1584\t| Built on the my-3D2dge handoff; engine changes allowed | §5, §6, `engine/CHANGES.md` | all suites |\n1585\t| Engine-native art plus new assets that feel like the source | §8.6, §9 | screens |\n1586\t| Engine animation plus new animations | §7.13 | filmstrips |\n1587\t| All audio in code; gibberish voices | §7.14–7.16 | `audio-data`, `audio-lint`, `voice` |\n1588\t| AI Hub multiplayer and the game on its own | §7.11, §7.12 | `hub`, `hub-harness`, `versus` |\n1589\t| Dev subfolder, bundled to the root | §4 | `bundle` |\n1590\t| Shared machine: no stray reads, own ports, own PIDs | §4.1 | — |\n1591\t| Performance unlimited, so add detail | §7.2, §9 | — |\n1592\t| Headless tests behind one command, with a whole-game playthrough | §12 | `run.mjs` |\n1593\t| A feature-complete, polished vertical slice showing all mechanics | §7, §8, §10 | playthrough suites |\n1594\t| Dev mode in the pause menu, with live sliders and reset | §7.23, Appendix A | `dev` |\n1595\t| Attract mode after 10 s idle; any input exits; cheats allowed | §7.22 | `attract` |\n1596\t| Narrative tone like the source | §8.11 | `content` |\n1597\t| 10-foot, controller-first; keyboard and mouse native; hover focus; PlayStation glyphs (Xbox option); prompts follow the last device | §7.17–7.20, Appendices B–C | `ui`, `glyphs` |\n1598\t| No dense web-style screens | §7.17 | `ui` readability |\n1599\t| Touch-only: no touch controls, the notice and Settings, attract runs | §7.21 | `touch`, `touch-emulation` |\n1600\t| No TODOs, placeholders or stubs | §4.3 lint, R6 | `content`, `bundle` |\n1601\t| Source Information and three feedback blocks in the reply and the About screen | §7.25 | `content` |\n1602\t| `exit-interview.json` in the exact shape, parsed | §7.25, M9 | `bundle` (parses it, checks every key is non-empty) |\n1603\t\n1604\t**Final steps (M9), in order:**\n1605\t1. Run `node tests/run.mjs` and get it green.\n1606\t2. Run `node build.mjs`.\n1607\t3. Fill `OB.ABOUT` with the real answers:\n1608\t   - **Date** and **Model:** from TASK.md.\n1609\t   - **Plan:** `PLAN.md`.\n1610\t   - **Usage:** say \"unknown\" if no estimate is available.\n1611\t   - **Feedback answers:** from the build log.\n1612\t4. Rebuild, so the About pages and `exit-interview.json` match.\n1613\t5. Validate the JSON: `node -e \"JSON.parse(require('fs').readFileSync('exit-interview.json','utf8'))\"`.\n1614\t6. Write the chat reply with the Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback.\n1615\t\n1616\tFill the `aiHub` block as follows:\n1617\t\n1618\t| Field | Value |\n1619\t|---|---|\n1620\t| `name` | \"OutBloom 2006: Coast 2 Coast\" |\n1621\t| `tagline` | \"One day. One road. Every flower in bloom.\" |\n1622\t| `type` | \"Game\" |\n1623\t| `genre` | \"Racing\" |\n1624\t| `source` | \"OutRun 2006: Coast 2 Coast\" |\n1625\t| `series` | \"OutRun\" |\n1626\t| `players` | \"1–4\" |\n1627\t| `controls` | \"Steer: stick/←→/A D · Gas: R2/✕/W · Brake: L2/□/S · Drift: ○/Space · Pause: Options/Esc\" |\n1628\t| `tags` | [\"arcade\", \"vehicles\", \"pixel art\", \"retro\", \"comedy\", \"versus\"] |\n1629\t\n1630\t---\n1631\t\n1632\t## Appendix A. Tuning defaults (the Dev Mode sliders)\n1633\t\n1634\tAll of these are live (`OB.tuning`), and `OB.TUNING_DEFAULTS` holds the defaults that reset restores. \"×\" means a\n1635\tmultiplier on the per-car or per-stage value.\n1636\t\n1637\t| Group | Key | Default | Range | Meaning |\n1638\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n1639\t| Car | `topSpeedScale` | 1.00 | 0.6–1.4 | × car top speed |\n1640\t| Car | `accelScale` | 1.00 | 0.5–2.0 | × car acceleration |\n1641\t| Car | `brakeDecel` | 240 | 120–400 | km/h per second at full brake |\n1642\t| Car | `steerRate` | 2.20 | 1.0–4.0 | half-roads per second at full lock (× car steer / 2.2) |\n1643\t| Car | `steerResponse` | 9 | 3–20 | per second; how fast lateral speed follows the stick |\n1644\t| Car | `curvePush` | 0.62 | 0.2–1.2 | the throw outward per curve unit at top speed |\n1645\t| Car | `offroadTop` | 0.50 | 0.2–1.0 | share of top speed off the asphalt |\n1646\t| Car | `offroadDrag` | 120 | 0–300 | km/h per second above the off-road top |\n1647\t| Drift | `driftMinSpeed` | 120 | 60–200 | km/h |\n1648\t| Drift | `driftTapWindow` | 0.35 | 0.1–0.8 | s, the longest brake tap that counts |\n1649\t| Drift | `driftGrip` | 0.85 | 0–1 | how much the drift cancels the curve push (× car drift grip / 0.85) |\n1650\t| Drift | `driftAngleMax` | 0.75 | 0.3–1.2 | rad |\n1651\t| Drift | `driftSpeedLoss` | 16 | 0–60 | km/h per second at full angle |\n1652\t| Drift | `driftSteerBoost` | 1.30 | 1–2 | × steering while drifting |\n1653\t| Slipstream | `slipRange` | 1800 | 600–3000 | units behind a vehicle |\n1654\t| Slipstream | `slipWidth` | 0.22 | 0.1–0.5 | half-roads |\n1655\t| Slipstream | `slipTopBonus` | 0.06 | 0–0.2 | × top speed at full slip |\n1656\t| Slipstream | `slingshotBoost` | 14 | 0–40 | km/h |\n1657\t| Traffic | `trafficDensity` | 1.00 | 0–3 | × stage density |\n1658\t| Traffic | `trafficSpeedScale` | 1.00 | 0.5–1.5 | × type speeds |\n1659\t| Traffic | `spinThreshold` | 70 | 20–150 | km/h of closing speed that spins you |\n1660\t| Traffic | `bumpSpeedLoss` | 0.08 | 0–0.5 | share of speed lost below the other car's |\n1661\t| Clock | `startTime` | 75 | 30–150 | s |\n1662\t| Clock | `checkpointBonus` | 58/57/56/55 | 20–120 each | s for rows 2–5 |\n1663\t| Rivals | `rivalSkill` | 1.00 | 0.5–1.5 | × rival top speed and cornering |\n1664\t| Rivals | `rivalRubber` | 0.50 | 0–1 | rubber-band strength |\n1665\t| Love in Bloom | `petalScale` | 1.00 | 0.5–2 | × petals per rating |\n1666\t| Love in Bloom | `requestDifficulty` | 1.00 | 0.5–1.5 | × request thresholds and counts |\n1667\t| Love in Bloom | `bloomDecay` | 4 | 1–10 | s before the chain drains |\n1668\t| Camera | `cameraHeight` | 1000 | 600–1600 | units (chase) |\n1669\t| Camera | `cameraFov` | 100 | 70–130 | degrees at rest |\n1670\t| World | `gameSpeed` | 1.00 | 0.25–2 | `game.timeScale` while racing |\n1671\t\n1672\t## Appendix B. Input map (defaults; every action rebindable on the Controls screen)\n1673\t\n1674\t| Action | Keyboard | Mouse | Pad (PlayStation · Xbox) |\n1675\t|---|---|---|---|\n1676\t| Steer | A / D, ← / → | — | left stick, d-pad ← → |\n1677\t| Gas (analog) | W, ↑ | — | R2 · RT (analog), ✕ · A |\n1678\t| Brake / reverse (analog) | S, ↓ | — | L2 · LT (analog), □ · X |\n1679\t| Drift (handbrake) | Space | — | ○ · B |\n1680\t| Shift up / down (manual) | E / Q (also Left Shift / Left Ctrl) | — | R1 / L1 · RB / LB |\n1681\t| Horn | H | — | △ · Y |\n1682\t| Camera | C | — | d-pad ↓ |\n1683\t| Radio: next station | R | — | d-pad ↑ |\n1684\t| Pause | Esc, P | — | Options · Menu |\n1685\t| Menus: move · select · back | arrows, WASD · Enter, Space · Esc, Backspace | hover · left click · right click | d-pad, stick · ✕ · A · ○ · B |\n1686\t| Menus: tab or page | Q / E | wheel | L1 / R1 · LB / RB |\n1687\t| Versus P2 (shared keyboard) | arrows to steer and gas or brake, `.` drift, `/` horn, `,` camera | — | the second pad |\n1688\t\n1689\t## Appendix C. Glyphs (`OB.UI.prompt`)\n1690\t\n1691\t| Engine label | PlayStation icon | Xbox icon | Keyboard and mouse |\n1692\t|---|---|---|---|\n1693\t| Pad0 | ✕ blue cross on a dark disc | A green disc | — |\n1694\t| Pad1 | ○ red ring | B red disc | — |\n1695\t| Pad2 | □ pink square | X blue disc | — |\n1696\t| Pad3 | △ green triangle | Y yellow disc | — |\n1697\t| Pad4 / 5 | L1 / R1 pills | LB / RB pills | — |\n1698\t| Pad6 / 7 | L2 / R2 trigger shapes | LT / RT trigger shapes | — |\n1699\t| Pad8 / 9 | Create / Options ovals | View / Menu ovals | — |\n1700\t| Pad12–15 | d-pad with the arm lit | the same | — |\n1701\t| left stick | an L stick with arrows | the same | — |\n1702\t| keys | — | — | a keycap with the key's label (W, ↑, Space, Esc, Enter…), wide caps for long names |\n1703\t| Mouse0 / 2 / wheel | — | — | a mouse with the left or right button lit, or the wheel |\n1704\t\n1705\tEvery icon is 12×12 at scale 2, with a 1-px dark outline so it reads on any background. The text label beside it is the\n1706\taction's name (\"DRIFT\").\n1707\t\n1708\t## Appendix D. The debug handle and events\n1709\t\n1710\t`game.debug`:\n1711\t- `snapshot()` returns `{ scene, mode, stage, seg, z, x, v, gear, rpm, drift: { on, a, score }, slip, air, crash,\n1712\t  clock, score, bloom: { level, points }, petals, grade, request: { type, state, progress }, route, goal, rivals:\n1713\t  [{ id, z, x, place }], traffic: n, attract: phase, hub: { active, phase, sent, progress }, ui: { screen, focus },\n1714\t  lastDevice, glyphs, errors: n }`.\n1715\t- `events(n)` returns the last n bus events.\n1716\t- `teleport(stage, pct, x)`.\n1717\t- `tune(key, v)` and `resetTuning()`.\n1718\t- `cheat(name, on)`.\n1719\t- `go(scene, data)`.\n1720\t- `bot(on, params)`.\n1721\t- `ui.press(action)` and `ui.click(x, y)`.\n1722\t\n1723\t`game.stateHash = () => [stage, z, x, v, clock, score, petals]`, rounded.\n1724\t\n1725\t**Bus events:**\n1726\t\n1727\t| Area | Events |\n1728\t|---|---|\n1729\t| run | `run:start`, `run:end {reason}`, `stage:enter {id}`, `checkpoint {id, bonus}`, `fork:approach {left, right}`, `fork:pref {side}`, `fork:choose {side, next}`, `goal {id, mood}`, `time:low`, `time:up` |\n1730\t| car | `car:bump {with}`, `car:crash {with}`, `car:scrape`, `car:offroad {on}`, `air:start`, `air:land {hard}`, `start:perfect`, `drift:start {dir}`, `drift:end {score, length}`, `slip:start`, `slip:end`, `slingshot`, `nearmiss {id}`, `overtake {id, kind}`, `gust`, `horn` |\n1731\t| Love in Bloom | `req:start {type}`, `req:progress {n, of}`, `req:end {type, rating, petals}`, `petals {delta, reason}`, `bloom:level {n}` |\n1732\t| objects | `obj:pop`, `obj:bonk`, `obj:sting`, `obj:snap`, `obj:cone`, `obj:arch`, `obj:bouquet`, `obj:photo {rating}` |\n1733\t| rivals and missions | `rival:pass {id, by}`, `rival:out {id}`, `mission:end {id, cleared, rank}` |\n1734\t| other | `radio:tune {i}`, `ui:screen {name}`, `attract:start`, `attract:end`, `hub:*` |\n1735\t\n1736\t## Appendix E. Research notes (what the plan relied on)\n1737\t\n1738\t- **The engine.** my-3D2dge v0.85.2 handoff: `README.md`, `API.md`, `AI_GUIDE.md` (the road-racing recipe, standard\n1739\t  features, testing, sessions), `docs/ASSETS.md` (shapes, `E.shapeSprite`), `modules/road.js` (the current single-road\n1740\t  renderer), `kits/road.js` (a complete coast-road slice: clock, checkpoints, radio, bot), `API-INDEX.md` (input,\n1741\t  glyphs, settings, audio, `E.Arcade`, `E.Road`). Its audio is a chip synth with one-shot voices and step-sequenced\n1742\t  songs on a game-time music clock. Its gamepad buttons are digital. Its standard menus are HTML.\n1743\t- **The AI Hub.** `lib/hub/README.md` (Session Protocol v1, client 1.4.0, modes, card, start, reporting, rules, the\n1744\t  harness checks), `examples/ghost.html` (the ghost pattern: send every 100 ms unreliable, progress once a second,\n1745\t  result on finish, a result on time-up) and `harness.html` (its checks and its synthetic key pokes).\n1746\t- **The source**, from Wikipedia and a moegamer retrospective. *OutRun 2006: Coast 2 Coast* (SEGA, developed by Sumo\n1747\t  Digital, 2006; PS2, PSP, Xbox, PC) has these modes and mechanics:\n1748\t  - OutRun, Heart Attack (on-the-fly passenger requests rated with heart points and a final grade), Time Attack, and\n1749\t    the Coast 2 Coast career, whose missions come from characters.\n1750\t  - Mission types including Test Your Drift, Test Your Slipstream, Avoid the Knockout and Don't Lose Your Girlfriend.\n1751\t  - Unlocks bought with \"OutRun Miles\", and up to 6 players online.\n1752\t  - A brake-tap drift and slipstreaming.\n1753\t  - The mood: blue skies and escapist fun.\n1754\t\n1755\t  Nothing here copies its content (§3).\n1756\t- **Not found, so don't claim it.** Exact request lists, stage counts per course set, the music track list. Write\n1757\t  \"unknown\" in Source Information for any such detail.\n1758\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6eff3345-97e6-4e65-b411-5b68a8aed193","uuid":"226e6140-0194-427c-97f5-493ea9b94351","timestamp":"2026-11-04T10:52:18.561Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build__20261104-035148__xe1b7e1/work/PLAN.md","content":"\n---\n\n## 11. Risks and how to de-risk them\n\n| # | Risk | Mitigation |\n|---|---|---|\n| R1 | **The fork renderer and the seamless stage switch.** The road module draws one road, so junctions are new code in the hottest path. | Build it first (M1). Keep it simple with the flat-junction rule (§7.1), one shared height profile and per-branch lateral offsets. Unit-test the projection: both branch centres; the road centre and player's screen x before and after the switch within 1 px; the gore cushion pushes to the nearer side. Record a filmstrip of a fork at 250 km/h, and review frames 1–16 for pops. |\n| R2 | **Drift and handling feel**, the heart of the game, tuned without hands on a pad. | Every constant is a live slider from M1. The physics suite asserts the design targets (§7.3: a 3 holdable flat out, a 4 needing a lift, a 6 drifted at about 85 %). **Balance windows**, asserted by the bot suite: on Normal, the reference bot finishes the all-left route with 15–30 s spare and the all-right route with 2–12 s. A \"sloppy\" bot (1 spin per stage) fails at least half the routes. A \"no-drift\" bot finishes no more than 6 of the 16. |\n| R3 | **Car sprites from shapes might look crude, or boot slowly.** | Prototype the Orchidea in M1 and check it with `asset-sheet.mjs` and in-race screenshots. If the shape renderer can't give clean curves at 128 px, draw the body with `px.poly` profiles per yaw band (the ROAD kit's method, 33 frames), which is just as complete. If boot exceeds about 8 s, render the cars a race needs behind a short \"Polishing the chrome…\" boot step at race start. |\n| R4 | **AI Hub conformance**, which is a hard gate. | Integrate in M3 using the ghost example's pattern. `ready()` never waits for input, and the client stays verbatim. Run the official harness in Playwright with 2 and 4 players, `latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5`, and the `host-leave` scenario. Re-run it on the final bundle (M9). |\n| R5 | **Audio quality, judged by builders who can't hear.** | Composition rules (§7.14), named instrument presets, and the offline render lint: in Playwright, `OfflineAudioContext` renders 12 s of each song through the real synth code. It asserts a peak under 0.98, RMS between 0.06 and 0.25, no silence over 1 s (except a designed rest), and band energy order (low < 250 Hz present, 1–4 kHz present). It also writes spectrogram PNGs to `.cache/audio/` to look at. |\n| R6 | **Content volume** (15 stages, 13 kits, 45 ending lines, 14 missions, 11 songs) causing gaps or placeholders. | Data schemas plus the **content-lint suite**: every stage has its fields, slots, landmarks and par; every goal has 3 moods × 3 passengers; every line key used exists and none is empty; every mission has rules, rewards and a bot; every bed has a cost and a reward source; bundle text has no TODO, placeholder or lorem. M2 lands all stages as real data early. |\n| R7 | **10-foot readability.** | UI-kit rules: a minimum scale and an item count, enforced in a debug-build assertion that the readability suite runs headless. Screenshots at 1920×1080 and 1280×720 reviewed in M7 and M8. The HUD's Extra-large size is tested too. |\n| R8 | **Headless speed and determinism.** | No pre-rendering headless. All gameplay randomness comes from seeded streams. `game.stateHash` covers the race state. A proof hash of 1,200 scripted steps run twice must match. |\n| R9 | **Split-screen layout.** | A per-scene `res` of `[640, 360]`, the rows layout, a HUD scaled for 180-px-tall viewports. Two-bot Versus test and screenshots. |\n| R10 | **Single-file bundle under `file://`.** | `build.mjs` lint (§4.3). The Playwright suite opens the root HTML by `file://`: no request leaves the page, there are no console errors, and the title is reached. |\n| R11 | **Touch-only false positives** (touchscreen laptops). | Require no fine pointer plus no keydown and no pad. Any key or pad clears the notice at once. Tested with emulation both ways. |\n| R12 | **Scope.** | Everything in this plan is in scope, and nothing more. Prefer finishing a system completely before starting the next, in milestone order. |\n\n---\n\n## 12. Testing\n\n### 12.1 The one command\n\n`node tests/run.mjs` runs everything from the dev folder:\n- the headless suites first (in about 10 s), then the browser suites;\n- `--quick` skips the browser suites, `--only <suite>[,<suite>]` runs only those, and `--update-par` rewrites the\n  stages' par and medal times from the reference bot.\n\n**Output.** One line per failure (`FAIL physics/curve-4-needs-lift: held 1.18 at 96% vtop`), then one summary line:\n`212 passed, 0 failed, 0 skipped (14.2 s)`. It exits with code 1 on any failure and prints nothing else.\n\n**The harness** (`tests/lib/boot.mjs`), as `game/test.mjs` does it:\n- Make a `vm` context with `window` (the context itself), `addEventListener`, `location` and `console` stand-ins.\n- Load `engine/my-3d2dge.js` and every file in `src/manifest.json`. The headless `E.Game` skips drawing.\n- Return `{ E, game, OB, I: game.input, snap: () => game.debug.snapshot(), events }`.\n- **Inputs:** `I.tap`, `I.hold`, `I.setAnalog` (E3), `I.feed` for the mouse. **Stepping:** `game.advance(s)`.\n- **Fresh state:** each suite boots a fresh context, so suites never share state.\n\n### 12.2 Headless suites\n\n| Suite | What it proves |\n|---|---|\n| `core` | boot reaches `title`; no `game.errors`; the bus delivers in order; tuning reset restores every default; rand streams repeat for a seed |\n| `road` | every stage builds within its length bounds; the first 150 segments and the junctions are flat; `info()` reports ground, edges and spans as the data says; the scenery is placed the same twice for one seed |\n| `junction` | branch centres at the decision point; the side chosen equals `sign(x)`; **seamless switch**: before and after it, the road centre's screen x is within 1 px and the player's z continues; gore cushion hits push to the nearer branch; a closed fork on a fixed route blocks that side |\n| `physics` | per-car top speed and 0–100 within 3 %; brakes; the curve-hold design targets; drift entry by all three inputs and none below 120 km/h; counter-steer straightens; drift score accumulates; the slipstream builds and the slingshot fires; off-road slowdown; rails scrape without spinning; bump against spin by closing speed; crest air and steering cut; gusts push |\n| `traffic` | the mix and density per stage; the minimum spacing; a two-lane gap always exists; lane changes avoid overlaps; junction branch assignment; ribbon pairs stay paired; overtake and near-miss detection with exact geometry cases; the horn makes the car ahead change lane |\n| `race` | the clock, checkpoint bonuses, difficulty scaling; time up → `gameover`; goal → `ending` → `results`; score maths; Bloom chain levels, decay and crash halving; the route string is recorded |\n| `playthrough-arcade` | **the bot drives all 16 routes to all 5 goals** on Normal within the R2 balance windows; no errors; every stage enters and every checkpoint fires once |\n| `requests` | **for each of the 14 types**, a scripted *pass* run (the bot targets the objects) rates Perfect, and a scripted *fail* run (drive straight in lane 1, or crash on purpose) rates Miss; petals and the favourite ×1.2; fork preference ±; penalties |\n| `playthrough-love` | Love in Bloom on 3 routes with 3 passengers reaches Bloom, Bud and Wilt (by scripted quality); every ending vignette runs to its end with every line found |\n| `missions` | **every mission** is cleared by its bot at C or better, and AAA is reachable with the \"perfect\" bot; the failure path works for each; rewards are paid once per rank improvement; unlock chains open the right postcards; the Finale opens at 9 cleared |\n| `economy` | 14 missions at A plus 6 arcade runs buy every bed; buying with too few seeds is refused; unlocks take effect in the garage and radio |\n| `trial` | a ghost records, saves, loads and replays to the same positions; splits; medals from par |\n| `versus` | two players join (pad and keyboard halves); a split-screen race with 2 bots finishes; places; rematch |\n| `hub` | with a **fake `AIHub`** in the context (`active: true`, `connect`, `ready`, `on`, `time`, `send`, `progress`, `result`, `answer`, `rng`), check: the title is skipped; `connect` uses ghost and `[1, 4]` with a card; `ready()` comes without input; Enter or ✕ on the card answers ready and △ answers more; the start syncs to `time() ≥ 0`; ghost sends happen at 8–12 Hz with `area`; progress comes once a second; the goal reports `result({ time })`; `time-up` reports `result({ score })` once; `end` stops input and sound; quit, restart and dev mode are hidden; Options with Create held doesn't pause. With `connect` resolving `null`, it boots to the title. |\n| `attract` | 10 s idle on each listed screen starts `attract`; 9.9 s doesn't; any key, pad, mouse move or click returns to the same screen and focus, and that input is consumed; a 100 s run emits fork, drift, request-Perfect, rival-or-versus and goal events; no profile writes |\n| `touch` | with stand-ins for touch-only media queries: the notice shows, the scenes have no touch buttons, and the attract mode runs; a tap doesn't end the attract mode; a keydown clears the notice |\n| `ui` | from the title, every screen in §5.3 is reachable with pad, keys and mouse (`I.feed`), and each has a way back; focus never lands on nothing; the readability assertion (text at scale 1 or more, at most 7 focusable items) holds on every screen |\n| `glyphs` | every action's prompt for keyboard, mouse, PlayStation and Xbox; the default is PlayStation; the setting switches to Xbox; rebinding changes the prompt; the device switch updates the prompts on the next step |\n| `dev` | the pause-menu Dev Mode item opens the panel; each slider changes its sim effect (for example, top speed changes v after 5 s of full gas); per-row and global reset; cheats work; every warp lands where it says; \"Keep after reload\" saves and restores |\n| `save` | the profile round-trip; a migration from a v0 fixture; blocked storage is survived; settings persist |\n| `voice` | the babble planner: syllable counts for fixtures, total duration in range, the same text giving the same plan, moods shifting pitch, punctuation pauses |\n| `audio-data` | every song parses (notes, chords, accents, slides); every track has an instrument; every song has a `sparkle` layer; every sound-effect and jingle name used exists |\n| `content` | the lint of R6: stages, goals, lines, missions, beds, songs, credits, `OB.ABOUT` fields all filled (no empty, \"TBD\" or placeholder strings) |\n| `determinism` | `proof()`: 1,200 scripted steps from a seed, hashed twice with the same result; the hash differs when the seed differs |\n\n### 12.3 Browser suites (Playwright, `tests/browser/`)\n\n| Suite | What it proves |\n|---|---|\n| `bundle` | `node build.mjs`, then open the **root HTML by `file://`** at 1920×1080: no console errors, no network requests, the title scene is reached, `game.debug.snapshot().scene === 'title'`; the lint of §4.3 |\n| `check` | `node engine/tools/check.mjs <root html>` exits 0; act on its look notes (and record them in the log) |\n| `hub-harness` | copy the bundle and `vendor/hub/*` into `.cache/hubtest/`; start `python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` in that folder; read the port it prints; confirm `/` serves our title; open `harness.html?game=<bundle>&players=2&auto=1`, then `players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1`, then `&scenario=host-leave`; each must reach `window.__hubCheck.pass === true`; kill the server by its PID |\n| `touch-emulation` | `check.mjs --size 390x844@3`: the notice is visible, no touch buttons are drawn, and the snapshot shows attract running after 11 s |\n| `audio-lint` | the offline render checks of R5 for every song and jingle |\n| `screens` | screenshots of title, menu, garage, radio, race (one per kit), a fork, Love in Bloom request, Tour map, garden, results, ending, Versus split, hub card and dev panel, at 1920×1080, into `.cache/shots/`, for the review in M7 and M8. This suite fails only on errors, never on looks. |\n\n### 12.4 What only eyes can check (screenshots and filmstrips)\n\nCheck these with `tools/filmstrip.mjs` or screenshots, and note them in the build log:\n- the drift and spin animation (filmstrip);\n- a fork at speed (filmstrip);\n- bloom and lens flare not smearing pixels;\n- night and tunnel tints;\n- the car layers and the occupants' alignment at every yaw (a contact sheet of 33 frames);\n- each kit's density and composition;\n- HUD legibility at 1280×720;\n- the hub card's look.\n\nEverything that can be a number, a state or an event is a script instead.\n\n---\n\n## 13. Definition of done: every TASK.md requirement and where it lives\n\n| TASK.md requirement | Where | Proved by |\n|---|---|---|\n| Genre and perspective that fit the source | §1, §7.2 | screens |\n| Title riffing on the name, picked by a real RNG | header | — |\n| Single HTML named per TASK.md; may use CDNs | §1 names, §4.3 (we need none) | `bundle` |\n| Built on the my-3D2dge handoff; engine changes allowed | §5, §6, `engine/CHANGES.md` | all suites |\n| Engine-native art plus new assets that feel like the source | §8.6, §9 | screens |\n| Engine animation plus new animations | §7.13 | filmstrips |\n| All audio in code; gibberish voices | §7.14–7.16 | `audio-data`, `audio-lint`, `voice` |\n| AI Hub multiplayer and the game on its own | §7.11, §7.12 | `hub`, `hub-harness`, `versus` |\n| Dev subfolder, bundled to the root | §4 | `bundle` |\n| Shared machine: no stray reads, own ports, own PIDs | §4.1 | — |\n| Performance unlimited, so add detail | §7.2, §9 | — |\n| Headless tests behind one command, with a whole-game playthrough | §12 | `run.mjs` |\n| A feature-complete, polished vertical slice showing all mechanics | §7, §8, §10 | playthrough suites |\n| Dev mode in the pause menu, with live sliders and reset | §7.23, Appendix A | `dev` |\n| Attract mode after 10 s idle; any input exits; cheats allowed | §7.22 | `attract` |\n| Narrative tone like the source | §8.11 | `content` |\n| 10-foot, controller-first; keyboard and mouse native; hover focus; PlayStation glyphs (Xbox option); prompts follow the last device | §7.17–7.20, Appendices B–C | `ui`, `glyphs` |\n| No dense web-style screens | §7.17 | `ui` readability |\n| Touch-only: no touch controls, the notice and Settings, attract runs | §7.21 | `touch`, `touch-emulation` |\n| No TODOs, placeholders or stubs | §4.3 lint, R6 | `content`, `bundle` |\n| Source Information and three feedback blocks in the reply and the About screen | §7.25 | `content` |\n| `exit-interview.json` in the exact shape, parsed | §7.25, M9 | `bundle` (parses it, checks every key is non-empty) |\n\n**Final steps (M9), in order:**\n1. Run `node tests/run.mjs` and get it green.\n2. Run `node build.mjs`.\n3. Fill `OB.ABOUT` with the real answers:\n   - **Date** and **Model:** from TASK.md.\n   - **Plan:** `PLAN.md`.\n   - **Usage:** say \"unknown\" if no estimate is available.\n   - **Feedback answers:** from the build log.\n4. Rebuild, so the About pages and `exit-interview.json` match.\n5. Validate the JSON: `node -e \"JSON.parse(require('fs').readFileSync('exit-interview.json','utf8'))\"`.\n6. Write the chat reply with the Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback.\n\nFill the `aiHub` block as follows:\n\n| Field | Value |\n|---|---|\n| `name` | \"OutBloom 2006: Coast 2 Coast\" |\n| `tagline` | \"One day. One road. Every flower in bloom.\" |\n| `type` | \"Game\" |\n| `genre` | \"Racing\" |\n| `source` | \"OutRun 2006: Coast 2 Coast\" |\n| `series` | \"OutRun\" |\n| `players` | \"1–4\" |\n| `controls` | \"Steer: stick/←→/A D · Gas: R2/✕/W · Brake: L2/□/S · Drift: ○/Space · Pause: Options/Esc\" |\n| `tags` | [\"arcade\", \"vehicles\", \"pixel art\", \"retro\", \"comedy\", \"versus\"] |\n\n---\n\n## Appendix A. Tuning defaults (the Dev Mode sliders)\n\nAll of these are live (`OB.tuning`), and `OB.TUNING_DEFAULTS` holds the defaults that reset restores. \"×\" means a\nmultiplier on the per-car or per-stage value.\n\n| Group | Key | Default | Range | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Car | `topSpeedScale` | 1.00 | 0.6–1.4 | × car top speed |\n| Car | `accelScale` | 1.00 | 0.5–2.0 | × car acceleration |\n| Car | `brakeDecel` | 240 | 120–400 | km/h per second at full brake |\n| Car | `steerRate` | 2.20 | 1.0–4.0 | half-roads per second at full lock (× car steer / 2.2) |\n| Car | `steerResponse` | 9 | 3–20 | per second; how fast lateral speed follows the stick |\n| Car | `curvePush` | 0.62 | 0.2–1.2 | the throw outward per curve unit at top speed |\n| Car | `offroadTop` | 0.50 | 0.2–1.0 | share of top speed off the asphalt |\n| Car | `offroadDrag` | 120 | 0–300 | km/h per second above the off-road top |\n| Drift | `driftMinSpeed` | 120 | 60–200 | km/h |\n| Drift | `driftTapWindow` | 0.35 | 0.1–0.8 | s, the longest brake tap that counts |\n| Drift | `driftGrip` | 0.85 | 0–1 | how much the drift cancels the curve push (× car drift grip / 0.85) |\n| Drift | `driftAngleMax` | 0.75 | 0.3–1.2 | rad |\n| Drift | `driftSpeedLoss` | 16 | 0–60 | km/h per second at full angle |\n| Drift | `driftSteerBoost` | 1.30 | 1–2 | × steering while drifting |\n| Slipstream | `slipRange` | 1800 | 600–3000 | units behind a vehicle |\n| Slipstream | `slipWidth` | 0.22 | 0.1–0.5 | half-roads |\n| Slipstream | `slipTopBonus` | 0.06 | 0–0.2 | × top speed at full slip |\n| Slipstream | `slingshotBoost` | 14 | 0–40 | km/h |\n| Traffic | `trafficDensity` | 1.00 | 0–3 | × stage density |\n| Traffic | `trafficSpeedScale` | 1.00 | 0.5–1.5 | × type speeds |\n| Traffic | `spinThreshold` | 70 | 20–150 | km/h of closing speed that spins you |\n| Traffic | `bumpSpeedLoss` | 0.08 | 0–0.5 | share of speed lost below the other car's |\n| Clock | `startTime` | 75 | 30–150 | s |\n| Clock | `checkpointBonus` | 58/57/56/55 | 20–120 each | s for rows 2–5 |\n| Rivals | `rivalSkill` | 1.00 | 0.5–1.5 | × rival top speed and cornering |\n| Rivals | `rivalRubber` | 0.50 | 0–1 | rubber-band strength |\n| Love in Bloom | `petalScale` | 1.00 | 0.5–2 | × petals per rating |\n| Love in Bloom | `requestDifficulty` | 1.00 | 0.5–1.5 | × request thresholds and counts |\n| Love in Bloom | `bloomDecay` | 4 | 1–10 | s before the chain drains |\n| Camera | `cameraHeight` | 1000 | 600–1600 | units (chase) |\n| Camera | `cameraFov` | 100 | 70–130 | degrees at rest |\n| World | `gameSpeed` | 1.00 | 0.25–2 | `game.timeScale` while racing |\n\n## Appendix B. Input map (defaults; every action rebindable on the Controls screen)\n\n| Action | Keyboard | Mouse | Pad (PlayStation · Xbox) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Steer | A / D, ← / → | — | left stick, d-pad ← → |\n| Gas (analog) | W, ↑ | — | R2 · RT (analog), ✕ · A |\n| Brake / reverse (analog) | S, ↓ | — | L2 · LT (analog), □ · X |\n| Drift (handbrake) | Space | — | ○ · B |\n| Shift up / down (manual) | E / Q (also Left Shift / Left Ctrl) | — | R1 / L1 · RB / LB |\n| Horn | H | — | △ · Y |\n| Camera | C | — | d-pad ↓ |\n| Radio: next station | R | — | d-pad ↑ |\n| Pause | Esc, P | — | Options · Menu |\n| Menus: move · select · back | arrows, WASD · Enter, Space · Esc, Backspace | hover · left click · right click | d-pad, stick · ✕ · A · ○ · B |\n| Menus: tab or page | Q / E | wheel | L1 / R1 · LB / RB |\n| Versus P2 (shared keyboard) | arrows to steer and gas or brake, `.` drift, `/` horn, `,` camera | — | the second pad |\n\n## Appendix C. Glyphs (`OB.UI.prompt`)\n\n| Engine label | PlayStation icon | Xbox icon | Keyboard and mouse |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Pad0 | ✕ blue cross on a dark disc | A green disc | — |\n| Pad1 | ○ red ring | B red disc | — |\n| Pad2 | □ pink square | X blue disc | — |\n| Pad3 | △ green triangle | Y yellow disc | — |\n| Pad4 / 5 | L1 / R1 pills | LB / RB pills | — |\n| Pad6 / 7 | L2 / R2 trigger shapes | LT / RT trigger shapes | — |\n| Pad8 / 9 | Create / Options ovals | View / Menu ovals | — |\n| Pad12–15 | d-pad with the arm lit | the same | — |\n| left stick | an L stick with arrows | the same | — |\n| keys | — | — | a keycap with the key's label (W, ↑, Space, Esc, Enter…), wide caps for long names |\n| Mouse0 / 2 / wheel | — | — | a mouse with the left or right button lit, or the wheel |\n\nEvery icon is 12×12 at scale 2, with a 1-px dark outline so it reads on any background. The text label beside it is the\naction's name (\"DRIFT\").\n\n## Appendix D. The debug handle and events\n\n`game.debug`:\n- `snapshot()` returns `{ scene, mode, stage, seg, z, x, v, gear, rpm, drift: { on, a, score }, slip, air, crash,\n  clock, score, bloom: { level, points }, petals, grade, request: { type, state, progress }, route, goal, rivals:\n  [{ id, z, x, place }], traffic: n, attract: phase, hub: { active, phase, sent, progress }, ui: { screen, focus },\n  lastDevice, glyphs, errors: n }`.\n- `events(n)` returns the last n bus events.\n- `teleport(stage, pct, x)`.\n- `tune(key, v)` and `resetTuning()`.\n- `cheat(name, on)`.\n- `go(scene, data)`.\n- `bot(on, params)`.\n- `ui.press(action)` and `ui.click(x, y)`.\n\n`game.stateHash = () => [stage, z, x, v, clock, score, petals]`, rounded.\n\n**Bus events:**\n\n| Area | Events |\n|---|---|\n| run | `run:start`, `run:end {reason}`, `stage:enter {id}`, `checkpoint {id, bonus}`, `fork:approach {left, right}`, `fork:pref {side}`, `fork:choose {side, next}`, `goal {id, mood}`, `time:low`, `time:up` |\n| car | `car:bump {with}`, `car:crash {with}`, `car:scrape`, `car:offroad {on}`, `air:start`, `air:land {hard}`, `start:perfect`, `drift:start {dir}`, `drift:end {score, length}`, `slip:start`, `slip:end`, `slingshot`, `nearmiss {id}`, `overtake {id, kind}`, `gust`, `horn` |\n| Love in Bloom | `req:start {type}`, `req:progress {n, of}`, `req:end {type, rating, petals}`, `petals {delta, reason}`, `bloom:level {n}` |\n| objects | `obj:pop`, `obj:bonk`, `obj:sting`, `obj:snap`, `obj:cone`, `obj:arch`, `obj:bouquet`, `obj:photo {rating}` |\n| rivals and missions | `rival:pass {id, by}`, `rival:out {id}`, `mission:end {id, cleared, rank}` |\n| other | `radio:tune {i}`, `ui:screen {name}`, `attract:start`, `attract:end`, `hub:*` |\n\n## Appendix E. Research notes (what the plan relied on)\n\n- **The engine.** my-3D2dge v0.85.2 handoff: `README.md`, `API.md`, `AI_GUIDE.md` (the road-racing recipe, standard\n  features, testing, sessions), `docs/ASSETS.md` (shapes, `E.shapeSprite`), `modules/road.js` (the current single-road\n  renderer), `kits/road.js` (a complete coast-road slice: clock, checkpoints, radio, bot), `API-INDEX.md` (input,\n  glyphs, settings, audio, `E.Arcade`, `E.Road`). Its audio is a chip synth with one-shot voices and step-sequenced\n  songs on a game-time music clock. Its gamepad buttons are digital. Its standard menus are HTML.\n- **The AI Hub.** `lib/hub/README.md` (Session Protocol v1, client 1.4.0, modes, card, start, reporting, rules, the\n  harness checks), `examples/ghost.html` (the ghost pattern: send every 100 ms unreliable, progress once a second,\n  result on finish, a result on time-up) and `harness.html` (its checks and its synthetic key pokes).\n- **The source**, from Wikipedia and a moegamer retrospective. *OutRun 2006: Coast 2 Coast* (SEGA, developed by Sumo\n  Digital, 2006; PS2, PSP, Xbox, PC) has these modes and mechanics:\n  - OutRun, Heart Attack (on-the-fly passenger requests rated with heart points and a final grade), Time Attack, and\n    the Coast 2 Coast career, whose missions come from characters.\n  - Mission types including Test Your Drift, Test Your Slipstream, Avoid the Knockout and Don't Lose Your Girlfriend.\n  - Unlocks bought with \"OutRun Miles\", and up to 6 players online.\n  - A brake-tap drift and slipstreaming.\n  - The mood: blue skies and escapist fun.\n\n  Nothing here copies its content (§3).\n- **Not found, so don't claim it.** Exact request lists, stage counts per course set, the music track list. Write\n  \"unknown\" in Source Information for any such detail.\n","numLines":279,"startLine":1480,"totalLines":1758}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_013jb3jHZrQd1zA23NrbLtSP","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
